  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"ahri","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Ahri"],"spikes":[{"when":"Level 2","text":"LeBlanc gets W-Q first and can slap Ahri for huge poke before Ahri has Charm online."},{"when":"Level 6","text":"LeBlanc's R-empowered chains-or-blink combo threatens a full kill before Ahri's Spirit Rush escape is even needed."},{"when":"Ahri level 6","text":"Ahri unlocks Spirit Rush, giving her the mobility and burst to punish a whiffed LeBlanc all-in and turn the fight around."},{"when":"Full build (Void Staff)","text":"Ahri's true-damage-scaling Orb of Deception plus Void Staff shreds LeBlanc's low magic resist even through her mobility."}],"wants":{"you":["Poke with Q-sigil autos","Punish pre-6 all-ins","Burst before Charm lands","Reset with W after kill threat"],"foe":["Farm safely under Charm threat","Wait for level 6 Spirit Rush","Play for late-game true damage scaling","Charm-lock LeBlanc's escape"]},"early":"LeBlanc dominates the first few levels because her Sigil-empowered Q hits like a truck while Ahri has no way to punish an aggressive trade before level 2. LeBlanc can shove and poke relentlessly, and if Ahri ever steps up without Charm ready, LeBlanc can chain-root and burst her down. Ahri has to play passively and farm with Orb of Deception, waiting for level 6 to feel safe.","mid":"Once both hit level 6, the matchup stays LeBlanc-favored through the R-doubled chains combo, letting her delete Ahri from range before Spirit Rush can be used defensively. LeBlanc should look for these all-in windows immediately on cooldown resets and roam using W's mobility when Ahri is warded up. Ahri's job here is to survive, farm under turret, and avoid overextending into chain range.","late":"In a fully scaled teamfight, Ahri's Orb of Deception scales into true damage and with a Void Staff she can burst LeBlanc down just as fast, while her own mobility via Spirit Rush makes her far harder to pin down than early game. LeBlanc's combo is telegraphed and if it whiffs or gets blocked she has almost nothing left, whereas Ahri's kit gives sustained repeatable damage. This late shift makes Ahri the safer, scarier late-game pick in a straight 1v1 or skirmish.","whys":["LeBlanc's Sigil-empowered Q outdamages anything Ahri has at levels 1-2, with no punish available from Ahri yet.","LeBlanc's poke and burst continue to outpace Ahri through levels 3-5 since Charm isn't reliably landing yet.","At level 6 LeBlanc's Mimic-doubled combo threatens a kill before Ahri's Spirit Rush is needed or even fully useful offensively.","With her first item, LeBlanc's burst window still outclasses Ahri's damage output in an all-in.","LeBlanc's roam and pick potential via W blink keeps her ahead in the mid-game skirmish phase.","LeBlanc's snowballed lead and burst combo still edges out Ahri heading into late game.","Ahri's true-damage-scaling Orb plus Spirit Rush mobility make her safer and stronger once both are fully built."]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"akali","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Akali"],"spikes":[{"when":"Level 2","text":"LeBlanc's Sigil-Q trade is stronger than anything Akali has before she gets Twilight Shroud stacked with Q."},{"when":"Level 6","text":"LeBlanc can chain-root Akali the instant she enters shroud, denying her the escape/burst window entirely."},{"when":"Akali level 6","text":"Akali's Perfect Execution R lets her dive through Chains and burst LeBlanc down if the root is already used."},{"when":"Akali 3-item (Shadowflame/Void Staff)","text":"Akali's shroud uptime and true-damage-adjacent scaling let her survive LeBlanc's combo and clean her up after."}],"wants":{"you":["Punish Akali pre-shroud","Save Chains for shroud dives","Poke down her energy/HP early","Avoid overextending post-6"],"foe":["Farm safely under shroud","Wait for level 6 dive threat","Bait Chains before diving","Snowball into late-game duels"]},"early":"LeBlanc wins this lane hard before level 6 because Akali has no reliable way to escape or punish a Sigil-empowered poke trade, and her energy costs make constant harassment risky. LeBlanc should shove hard and threaten all-ins any time Akali steps forward without Shroud up. Akali is forced into a passive farming pattern, just soaking XP and waiting for her ultimate.","mid":"LeBlanc keeps the advantage into her first item and through level 6 as long as she holds Ethereal Chains in reserve to root Akali the instant she commits to Shroud, shutting down the dive before it starts. This is her best window to solo kill Akali repeatedly and snowball the roam game. Akali's counterplay is to bait the root out with a fake approach before truly diving.","late":"Once Akali gets a couple of items, particularly anything with true damage or grievous wounds counters aside, her Shroud uptime and reset potential from Perfect Execution let her survive an initial LeBlanc burst and then cleanly finish the trade. LeBlanc's combo is a one-shot-or-nothing proposition, and a fed Akali with escape tools can dodge chains and punish the whiff. This makes late-game skirmishes swing back in Akali's favor despite her rough laning phase.","whys":["LeBlanc's Sigil-Q trade beats Akali's energy-limited poke at levels 1-2 with no punish available.","LeBlanc continues to out-trade Akali through levels 3-5 since Shroud isn't strong enough yet to protect dives.","At level 6 LeBlanc can pre-emptively root Akali the instant she enters Shroud, denying the dive combo entirely.","LeBlanc's first item burst still lets her punish Akali's commitments before her damage output catches up.","LeBlanc's chain-lockdown on Shroud dives keeps her ahead through the mid-game skirmish phase.","LeBlanc's snowballed roam pressure and burst combo remain favored heading into late game.","Akali's true-damage scaling and Perfect Execution resets let her survive LeBlanc's burst and clean up the trade once fully built."]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"akshan","win":["Skill","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Akshan"],"spikes":[{"when":"Level 1-2","text":"Both champions rely on landing skillshots/autos at range with no clear kit advantage, making early trades purely mechanical."},{"when":"Level 6","text":"LeBlanc's R-doubled Chains lets her lock down and burst Akshan before he can use Comeuppance or dash away with Heroic Swing."},{"when":"Akshan level 6","text":"Akshan's Comeuppance threatens burst plus revive-utility for allies, giving him a reason to all-in confidently."},{"when":"Akshan 3-item (crit/on-hit)","text":"Akshan's scaling autos and Twist the Knife stacks out-damage LeBlanc's burst if her combo doesn't one-shot him outright."}],"wants":{"you":["Win trades with clean Q-sigil pokes","Avoid predictable dash angles","Burst him down at 6 before he reacts","Deny his ward/camouflage plays"],"foe":["Farm safely behind minions with camouflage","Look for takedown resets","Wait to scale into crit items","Punish LeBlanc's all-in with Swing"]},"early":"This lane is a genuine skill matchup at levels 1-2 since neither side has a decisive kit advantage yet, with both champions poking at range and relying on landing skillshots cleanly. Whoever plays cooldowns and positioning better typically wins these trades. From level 3 onward LeBlanc's Sigil-Q combo starts pulling ahead as she can layer burst faster than Akshan can respond.","mid":"At level 6, LeBlanc's Mimic-doubled Ethereal Chains gives her a much more reliable lockdown than anything Akshan has, letting her land the full burst combo before he can use Heroic Swing to escape. She should look to all-in the moment she has R up and he's out of position. Akshan's camouflage from being near minions or through Missing Persons makes picking him off in the fog trickier, but a landed root usually spells doom for him early.","late":"With a full build, Akshan's consistent auto-attack damage, on-hit effects, and revive potential from Comeuppance make him a genuine threat in extended fights, especially since LeBlanc's all-in becomes more predictable and interruptible as game knowledge increases. If LeBlanc's burst doesn't kill him outright, Akshan's sustained damage and repositioning tools let him turn the fight around. This makes the late-game teamfight phase lean back toward Akshan despite LeBlanc's dominant mid-game window.","whys":["Neither champion holds an inherent kit advantage at levels 1-2, making it purely a skill matchup based on trades and positioning.","LeBlanc's Sigil-Q combo starts to pull ahead through levels 3-5 as she can layer burst faster than Akshan can respond.","At level 6, LeBlanc's Mimic-doubled Chains gives her a much more reliable lockdown than Akshan has to escape or punish.","LeBlanc's first item burst window lets her all-in and kill Akshan before Heroic Swing can save him.","LeBlanc's roam and pick pressure via chain lockdown keeps her ahead through the mid-game phase.","LeBlanc's snowballed lead and burst reliability remain favored heading into the late game.","Akshan's scaling auto-attacks, on-hit damage, and revive-capable ultimate let him out-duel LeBlanc once fully itemized."]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"anivia","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Anivia"],"spikes":[{"when":"Level 2","text":"LeBlanc's Sigil-Q burst punishes Anivia hard before Anivia has Flash Frost stun reliably landing and before her Wall is up."},{"when":"Level 6","text":"LeBlanc's doubled chains lets her burst Anivia down before Glacial Storm can zone her off or before Rebirth even matters much."},{"when":"Anivia level 6","text":"Anivia's Rebirth passive means she can survive a first burst attempt and punish LeBlanc's spent cooldowns on the second life."},{"when":"Anivia 4-item (Zhonya's/Rylai's/Void Staff)","text":"Anivia's late-game teamfight zoning with Wall plus Storm plus Rebirth makes her nearly impossible to burst down and delete for good."}],"wants":{"you":["Punish Anivia before Flash Frost is up","Burst around Rebirth's second life","Deny Wall zoning with W blink","Snowball roams off early kills"],"foe":["Farm safely with Frostbite poke","Wall off LeBlanc's engage","Survive on Rebirth then punish","Scale into unkillable teamfight zoning"]},"early":"LeBlanc's mobility and burst let her dictate this lane hard in the early levels, since Anivia is slow, has no dash, and is very vulnerable to a Sigil-Q all-in before her passive egg even matters if LeBlanc simply avoids the immediate kill and lets her walk off. LeBlanc should abuse her W blink to dodge Flash Frost and punish Anivia's positioning constantly. Anivia is stuck farming passively with Frostbite poke and hoping to survive to scale.","mid":"Through level 6 and into her first item, LeBlanc continues to threaten burst kills, using Mimic'd Chains to lock Anivia in place before she can Wall off the engage, though she must be mindful that Rebirth grants a second life that can punish a spent combo. The best play is to burst, retreat via W, and finish the egg with follow-up or wait for the passive to expire. Anivia's counterplay is to Wall proactively and poke from max range rather than trading in melee-ish range.","late":"In the late game, Anivia's full kit comes online as a nightmarish teamfight zoning tool: Wall splits the fight, Glacial Storm slows and shreds anyone caught in it, and Rebirth means she often needs to be killed twice to truly die. LeBlanc's single burst combo, even doubled by Mimic, frequently isn't enough to kill through the egg and reposition, and if she whiffs into a walled-off position she has nothing left. This makes Anivia the much scarier late-game threat in extended fights and objectives.","whys":["LeBlanc's mobility and Sigil-Q burst punish Anivia hard at levels 1-2 before Flash Frost or Wall are reliably usable.","LeBlanc continues to out-trade the slow, escape-less Anivia through levels 3-5.","At level 6, LeBlanc's Mimic-doubled Chains lets her burst Anivia down before Glacial Storm or Rebirth become relevant defensively.","LeBlanc's first item burst still threatens to kill through Anivia's passive egg with follow-up.","LeBlanc's W blink lets her dodge Wall zoning and keep picking Anivia off through the mid-game.","LeBlanc's snowballed lead and burst reliability remain favored heading into late game.","Anivia's Wall, Glacial Storm zoning, and Rebirth's second life make her nearly unkillable and dominant once fully itemized."]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"annie","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Annie"],"spikes":[{"when":"Level 2","text":"LeBlanc out-trades Annie before Pyromania is stacked to four spells, since the Sigil-Q burst hits first and harder from 700 range."},{"when":"Level 6","text":"LeBlanc's doubled Chains root shuts down Annie's stun-into-burst combo before she can even react with Tibbers."},{"when":"Annie level 6","text":"Annie's Tibbers gives her a stun-summon combo that threatens an instant kill on LeBlanc if her passive stun is up."},{"when":"Annie 3-item (Zhonya's/Void Staff)","text":"Annie's fully stacked stun-into-Tibbers-into-full-rotation is nearly guaranteed burst that outpaces LeBlanc's damage output."}],"wants":{"you":["Trade before her passive stacks","Root her before her stun combo starts","Poke safely from range with Q-sigil","Punish mana-hungry Annie early"],"foe":["Farm and stack passive safely","Land stun into Tibbers combo","Play around Zhonya's timing","Wait for full-build one-shot potential"]},"early":"LeBlanc holds a clear edge in the early levels since Annie needs to land four spells to stack her passive stun, and LeBlanc's Sigil-Q trade simply deals more damage faster without a setup requirement. LeBlanc should punish Annie hard whenever her passive is on cooldown, since Annie has no escape and is extremely squishy. Annie is forced to play safe and farm carefully to avoid feeding easy kills.","mid":"LeBlanc's advantage continues into level 6 and her first item because her Mimic-empowered Chains can lock Annie down and burst her before Annie's own stun-Tibbers combo comes online, especially if LeBlanc baits the passive stun off cooldown first. This is her strongest all-in window and she should press it aggressively. Annie's job is to survive to the point where her stacked passive plus Tibbers makes trades scarier.","late":"Once Annie hits a full build with Zhonya's Hourglass to survive dives and enough AP to guarantee a stun-into-Tibbers-into-full-rotation kill, she becomes one of the scariest one-shot burst mages in the game, capable of deleting LeBlanc in under a second if the stun lands. LeBlanc's own combo requires her spells to connect and if she's stunned first she has no way to fight back. This late-game burst race tips clearly in Annie's favor in a straight skirmish.","whys":["LeBlanc's Sigil-Q burst out-trades Annie at levels 1-2 since Annie needs multiple spells to stack her passive stun first.","LeBlanc continues to punish Annie through levels 3-5 while her passive stun remains unreliable.","At level 6, LeBlanc's Mimic-doubled Chains shuts down Annie's stun-into-Tibbers combo before it can be used.","LeBlanc's first item burst window lets her all-in and kill Annie before her own combo comes online.","LeBlanc's ability to bait Annie's passive stun off cooldown keeps her ahead through the mid-game.","LeBlanc's snowballed lead and burst reliability remain favored heading into late game.","Annie's fully stacked stun-into-Tibbers combo becomes a guaranteed one-shot that outpaces LeBlanc's own burst once fully itemized."]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"aurelionsol","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Aurelion Sol"],"spikes":[{"when":"Level 2","text":"LeBlanc's mobility and burst let her dive in and punish Aurelion Sol before Cosmic Creator has banked any Stardust for him."},{"when":"Level 6","text":"LeBlanc's doubled Chains root cancels his Breath of Light channel and lets her burst him before Singularity can pull her in."},{"when":"AS level 6","text":"Aurelion Sol's Falling Star stun combined with Singularity's pull threatens to lock LeBlanc down for a big trade if she engages carelessly."},{"when":"AS 4-item (Rocketbelt/Void Staff)","text":"A scaled Aurelion Sol turns into an unkillable poke-and-zone machine whose Stardust-fed Breath of Light outlasts anything LeBlanc's burst can do."}],"wants":{"you":["Dive before Stardust stacks","Dodge Breath of Light poke with W","Burst him down before Singularity pull","Deny his passive stacking with pressure"],"foe":["Farm safely with Breath of Light","Land Falling Star stun","Zone with Singularity pull","Scale into unkillable poke-mage late game"]},"early":"LeBlanc dominates the early lane because Aurelion Sol is slow, Astral Flight is 22 seconds at rank 1, and Breath of Light needs a long channel to hurt, meaning LeBlanc can dive in with W-E-Q and punish him severely. She should abuse her mobility to break that channel and continually threaten all-ins whenever his cooldowns are down. Aurelion Sol is stuck farming passively, hoping Stardust stacks fast enough to poke back.","mid":"Through level 6 and her first item, LeBlanc keeps the pressure on by using Mimic'd Chains to shut down his stun-and-pull combo before he can lock her in place with Singularity, letting her land clean burst kills. This is her best window to snowball since Aurelion Sol has very little defensive tech besides positioning. His only real counterplay is to stay maximally spaced and poke from as far as possible.","late":"Once Aurelion Sol gets a couple of items and Stardust piles up, he becomes an incredibly safe and oppressive poke-and-zone mage whose Breath of Light and Singularity can shred anyone who approaches, all while Astral Flight gives him a 1500-range escape over terrain. LeBlanc's burst combo requires getting in range, which is exactly where the black hole punishes her hardest, and if her all-in whiffs she has no follow-up. This makes the late-game teamfight and skirmish phase favor Aurelion Sol's safer, scaling kit.","whys":["LeBlanc's mobility and burst let her dive Aurelion Sol at levels 1-2 before his stars deal meaningful damage.","LeBlanc continues to punish the slow, escape-less Aurelion Sol through levels 3-5.","At level 6, LeBlanc's Mimic-doubled Chains shuts down his stun-and-pull combo before Singularity can lock her down.","LeBlanc's first item burst window still lets her all-in and kill him before his stars stack up threateningly.","LeBlanc's Distortion dodges Breath of Light's channel and keeps her ahead through the mid-game.","LeBlanc's snowballed lead and burst reliability remain favored heading into late game.","Aurelion Sol's massive scaled stars and safe zoning poke let him out-duel LeBlanc from range once fully itemized."]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"aurora","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Aurora"],"spikes":[{"when":"Level 2","text":"LeBlanc's Sigil-Q burst out-trades Aurora before Aurora has stacked enough passive procs or landed her E-slow reliably."},{"when":"Level 6","text":"LeBlanc's doubled chains lets her lock Aurora down and burst her before she can kite away with her mobility-heavy kit."},{"when":"Aurora level 6","text":"Aurora's ultimate gives her a translocate-and-reposition tool that can save her from a bad fight or set up a counter-engage."},{"when":"Aurora 3-item (Shadowflame/Void Staff)","text":"Aurora's sustained poke, the spirits healing her through chip damage, and her hops out-duel LeBlanc in long skirmishes."}],"wants":{"you":["Punish before her passive stacks","Burst before she can kite away","Deny her E-slow zoning","Snowball early kills into roams"],"foe":["Farm safely with poke spacing","Heal up on Spirit Abjuration","Kite with mobility tools","Scale into sustained-damage teamfights"]},"early":"LeBlanc's early burst and mobility give her the edge in the first few levels since Aurora needs to land poke and build up her passive stacks before her damage becomes threatening, while LeBlanc can simply all-in with Sigil-Q before that engine gets going. LeBlanc should punish aggressive trades and deny Aurora easy farm whenever possible. Aurora is forced to play at range and slow-walk her way into relevance.","mid":"LeBlanc continues to hold the advantage through level 6 and her first item spike, using Mimic'd chains to root Aurora down before her kit's mobility lets her disengage, securing clean kills in skirmishes. This is LeBlanc's peak power window and she should look to snowball aggressively. Aurora's best play is to poke from max range and avoid committing to close-range trades until she has more tools online.","late":"As Aurora scales into a full build, her sustained poke damage, the healing from Spirit Abjuration, and her repositioning hops let her outlast and outduel LeBlanc in extended fights, especially since LeBlanc's all-in is a single burst window that can be dodged or survived with enough mobility. If LeBlanc's combo doesn't secure a kill outright, Aurora's kit lets her turn the fight around with sustained damage. This makes the late-game skirmish phase favor Aurora's more resilient scaling kit.","whys":["LeBlanc's Sigil-Q burst out-trades Aurora at levels 1-2 before her passive stacks build up.","LeBlanc continues to punish Aurora through levels 3-5 while her damage engine is still coming online.","At level 6, LeBlanc's Mimic-doubled Chains lets her lock Aurora down before her mobility tools can be used to escape.","LeBlanc's first item burst window still lets her all-in and kill Aurora before her sustained poke becomes threatening.","LeBlanc's snowballing kill pressure keeps her ahead through the mid-game skirmish phase.","LeBlanc's snowballed lead and burst reliability remain favored heading into late game.","Aurora's sustained poke, the healing from Spirit Abjuration, and her hops let her outlast LeBlanc's single burst window once itemized."]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"azir","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Your kill window is any time Shifting Sands (22s at rank 1) is spent — E-Q-auto-W-auto deletes him before a fresh soldier can be readied for him to dash to."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Mimic combo (E-Q-W-R) one-shots an Azir who over-shoves; but Emperor's Divide is a 120s knockback wall — bait it out before you commit."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Lost Chapter into Luden's keeps your burst ahead of the shield on Shifting Sands; his soldier range makes him feel safe further out than he is."},{"when":"Late","text":"Emperor's Divide is terrain that knocks you back and walls off your W dash, and his soldier poke out-ranges your 700 Q, so late teamfights swing back to him if you don't close it earlier."}],"wants":{"you":["Punish any wave he pushes up while his E is down","Bait his soldier placement then W past it and back","Snowball the lane kill into a mid roam before his passive wall matters"],"foe":["Survive to 9 for Shifting Sands mobility","Farm safely under turret with soldiers as a wall","Stall to scale into 3-item teamfight relevance"]},"early":"Azir is one of the most fragile early laners in the game — Conquering Sands is 14s at rank 1, his clear leans on soldiers he must keep re-summoning, and he folds the moment he is caught out of position. Your E-Q poke chunks him every time he steps up to farm, and the sigil detonation finishes anything he tries to walk away from. All-in at 3 the moment he oversteps his soldier's 525 range; with Shifting Sands spent he has no engage and no escape for 22 seconds.","mid":"This is where you close it out. Level 6 E-Q-W into the mimic burns through the Shifting Sands shield if he's pushed even a little, and Arise! soldiers cannot body-block your W the way they wall off a melee diver. Keep shoving him into turret with your superior clear speed, then roam — Azir clears slowly without stacked soldiers and his 120s Emperor's Divide is his only answer to a collapse. Ward his flanks since his only real threat pre-6 is a jungle gank.","late":"Once Azir hits his mid-game item spike (Nashor's Tooth into a Rabadon's/Liandry's core) and keeps soldiers stacked, his poke and zone control start to catch up to your burst — Emperor's Divide is terrain that knocks you back and cuts your W path out. You want to end the game before he's a 3-item soldier fortress; if it drags to late you need picks pre-teamfight rather than diving his backline through a soldier wall.","whys":["He has no soldier up at level 1 and Conquering Sands is 14s, so your E-Q poke is free","Same story at 2 — the soldier does his damage, but Conquering Sands on 14s leaves long gaps","At 3 your chain holds him inside his own soldier line and his 22s Shifting Sands is the only way out","Shifting Sands is 22s at rank 1 — once he dashes to a soldier he has no exit for a full wave","Your burst still outpaces his damage even after his level 6 spike since his kit adds no new threat to you","Item lead compounds your burst advantage faster than his scaling poke can catch up","Even at 2+ items your assassination combo into a squishy mage still lands first if you engage on your terms"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"brand","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Sear is 8s at rank 1 and only stuns you while Blaze is ticking — W in on the whiff, detonate the sigil, and return before he can re-burn you."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"His Pillar of Flame + Conflagration combo hurts but he still has no gap-close, so your R-E-Q-W burst kills him before he can land a full rotation back."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Luden's Companion widens your burst window further than his Liandry's ramping damage; you kill him in one combo before his passive DoT ever ticks meaningfully."},{"when":"Late","text":"Brand's Blaze-into-Sear stun becomes a real teamfight threat once he's stacked Rylai's/Liandry's and a 100s Pyroclasm has five bounces to find, so pick him off pre-fight rather than letting him open from a flank."}],"wants":{"you":["Punish every missed Sear with a W dash-in and return","All-in while Blaze is off you and Sear is down","Snowball early kills into shutting down his scaling DoT power"],"foe":["Land Q pokes from max range without overextending","Farm to 2-item Liandry's/Rylai's spike","Light Blaze first, then land Sear to stun"]},"early":"Brand has zero mobility and a skillshot-reliant kit, which is exactly what your dash punishes. Bait his Sear, and the instant it whiffs or is on its 8s cooldown you W onto him for an uncontested trade — he has no escape and no shield, and Sear cannot stun you unless Blaze is already on you. Play forward from level 3 since he can't threaten anything back once you're inside his 625 E range.","mid":"Your burst combo at 6 (R-E-Q-W) kills him outright before his own combo (Q-W-E) can fully land back, since he needs to land three separate skillshots/AAs while you just need one clean engage. Keep pressuring his farm and denying his passive stacks — a starved Brand mid never becomes the teamfight threat he wants to be. Roam off advantage since he has no wave-clear pressure to punish you leaving.","late":"Brand becomes genuinely dangerous in 5v5s once he has Liandry's/Rylai's/Zhonya's — Pillar of Flame into Sear stuns anyone Blaze has already lit, and a 100s Pyroclasm bounces five times through a grouped team. The read is to keep denying his lane presence so he never gets there uncontested, and to burst him in the pick phase before a fight starts rather than letting him spark one.","whys":["No dash on Brand means you pick every level 1 trade, and Sear cannot stun you unburnt","Same story through level 5 — his only tools are skillshots you can just dash past","At 3 your chain roots him for the full string and he has no dash to break the 1.5s tether","Sear only stuns once Blaze is on you, so open the trade and never eat his W first","Mimic makes your rotation one-shot; his Pyroclasm is 100s and wants you standing still","Your snowballed lead keeps him starved of the DoT stacks he needs to matter later","Even scaled, a pick before the fight beats a Pyroclasm that needs five bounces to matter"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"cassiopeia","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Cassiopeia"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Cassiopeia has no dash and a 24s Miasma before her full combo is online — W in the moment that cloud is spent and she cannot answer, since Serpentine Grace only helps her once her poison is already on you."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Petrify (R) only lands if you're already poisoned and facing her, so your burst combo (R-E-Q-W) still kills her first if you engage from an angle rather than head-on."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Luden's burst still outpaces her Rylai's poison ramp-up; the fight is decided in the first two seconds, before her DoT ever ticks enough to matter."},{"when":"Late","text":"Once she's stacked Rabadon's/Liandry's/Zhonya's, Miasma grounds you out of your own escape and an 80s Petrifying Gaze stuns everyone facing her, so late she out-scales your burst if the game drags."}],"wants":{"you":["W in the instant Miasma is spent and cannot ground you","Trade before she has full Twin Fang reset chains online","Snowball your early kill lead into shutting down her scaling before 3 items"],"foe":["Poke safely with Q from max range without stepping into E-range","Stack poison for the movement passive to kite you","Survive to a 2-3 item spike where her DoT and Petrify one-shot"]},"early":"Cassiopeia's early kit is deceptively soft — no dash, and Twin Fang deals its real damage only into already-poisoned targets, so her output ramps over multiple hits rather than bursting you like you burst her. W in while she's still building poison and she has no way to disengage, since Serpentine Grace only helps once her poison is already on you. Look for all-ins at 3-5 while Miasma is still 24s and cannot ground your exit.","mid":"Your combo at 6 still wins if you approach from a flank angle her Petrify facing-cone can't catch, since R-E-Q-W kills her before her own slower poison-stack combo finishes. Keep denying her farm and forcing bad trades — a Cassiopeia behind in items has none of the raw one-shot power she needs later. Roam when you can; she has no way to punish a shoved wave without her own dash.","late":"This matchup flips hard once she's 2-3 items deep — Rylai's/Liandry's poison plus an 80s Petrifying Gaze on anyone facing her means one rotation takes your whole bar. Your job is to have ended the game or built a big enough lead before this point; if it goes late you need to engage from unusual angles and never let her face you directly in a poisoned state.","whys":["No dash and slow poison ramp-up means she loses every early trade you open with Q-W","Same at 2 — Miasma is 24s at rank 1 and one cloud cannot cover a whole lane","Your dash and burst still outclass her ramping poison combo pre-6","Angled engage lets your burst combo kill her before Petrify ever lines up","Item lead keeps your one-rotation burst ahead of her slower DoT-based combo","Snowballing the lead denies her the farm needed to reach her power items","At full build her Petrify+poison output finally out-scales your burst if she catches you facing her"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"diana","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Skill","Skill","Diana","Diana","Diana"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Diana's Crescent Strike is 8s at rank 1 and Lunar Rush cannot reach you without it — dodge the arc, W in, and take the trade while Pale Cascade is still down."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Lunar Rush (22s at rank 1) is her engage well before 6; Moonfall then adds a 100s pull that starts the fight for her, so commit only once Crescent Strike has already whiffed."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Luden's burst outpaces her early Riftmaker before she gets tankier; punish this window hard since it's your last clean kill window before she starts surviving your combo."},{"when":"Late","text":"Once Diana has Riftmaker/Zhonya's stacked, her R-pull into a full team engage combined with the omnivamp/true-damage ramp means she can dive your backline and survive your entire burst — this matchup flips late."}],"wants":{"you":["Dodge Crescent Strike then punish the 8s cooldown with W in","All-in aggressively before level 6 gives her a forced engage tool","Close the game before she completes a bruiser-jungle-style item spike"],"foe":["Land Q from range to set up an all-in","Survive to 6 for Moonfall engage/disengage utility","Scale into a Riftmaker/Zhonya's build that outlasts your burst"]},"early":"Diana before 6 is Crescent Strike on 8s, a 15s Pale Cascade shield, and Lunar Rush from level 3 — and Lunar Rush only reaches you if Crescent Strike marked you first, so the arc is the whole fight. Dodge it and you get a clean W trade with nothing she can do about it; her Moonsilver Blade third hit adds up, so don't chain-trade at her range. Build the whole lead in this window, before Moonfall comes online at 6.","mid":"At 6 she gains Moonfall, a 100s pull and slow that lets her force an engage onto you or peel for allies — this is the point the matchup turns over to her. Your burst still kills her if you catch Crescent Strike down, but she punishes back with a full R-Q-W rotation the moment the pull lands. Play around her all-in windows here rather than diving freely.","late":"This is where Diana genuinely takes over — a completed Riftmaker gives her sustained true damage and tankiness that lets her survive your burst and kill you back through the fight, and her ultimate becomes a teamfight-defining engage with Pale Cascade, 9s at rank 5, absorbing your poke on top. You want this game over before her second item; if it drags, play from range and never stand inside Moonfall's 475.","whys":["At 1 she is melee with only Crescent Strike on 8s, so your ranged sigil poke is free","Same at 2 — Pale Cascade shields the trade, but she still has no way to close the gap on you","Lunar Rush arrives at 3 on 22s — dodge Crescent Strike and it has nothing to dash to","Even exchanges: her shield eats your poke, your chain answers every Lunar Rush she throws","Moonfall pulls you into her whole rotation, and a 100s ultimate only needs to land once","Her first item buys enough sustain to live through your combo and then kill you back for it","Once she's tanky and dealing true damage her sustain outlasts your all-in and she wins extended fights"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"ekko","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Ekko"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Timewinder is 9s at rank 1 and damages again on its return pass, so W in after the grenade has rewound past you — then leave before his anomaly forms."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Chronobreak is 110s at rank 1 and makes him untargetable while it resolves — your full string kills him outright when it is down, and is thrown away entirely when it is not, so check it before you open."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Luden's/first-item burst punishes him hard since his own core item timing (Riftmaker) needs more gold to come online than your one-item power spike."},{"when":"Late","text":"A completed Riftmaker plus his ultimate rewind gives him a near-unkillable dive-and-reset combo in fights, meaning he can finally trade into your burst and survive to punish you back."}],"wants":{"you":["Punish every Timewinder throw with an immediate W in","All-in before he has Chronobreak up as an escape","Snowball early kills into denying his mid-game Riftmaker timing"],"foe":["Land Q to slow/poke from range without over-extending","Survive to 6 for a Chronobreak safety net","Farm to a Riftmaker spike that lets him dive and sustain through fights"]},"early":"Ekko's Timewinder is 9s at rank 1 and comes back through you on the return pass, so W in once the grenade has already rewound past you and the trade is uncontested — his shield sits on Parallel Convergence and only exists if he walks into his own anomaly. Look for all-ins from level 3 whenever Timewinder is down; Phase Dive is only 325 range, so it closes gaps but never escapes yours.","mid":"At 6 he gets Chronobreak, a 110s rewind that heals a share of the damage he just took — and he is untargetable while it resolves, so a last spell thrown into it is simply gone. Keep applying pressure and denying his farm; a starved Ekko never reaches the Riftmaker timing he needs to become a threat. Roam off any lane lead since he has no wave-clear tool to punish your movement.","late":"This is where Ekko turns dangerous — once he has Riftmaker's omnivamp and a fully-available Chronobreak, he can dive onto your backline, take the trade, and rewind out or sustain through your damage entirely. You want to end this game before his second item; if it drags, you need to bait Chronobreak out with a fake engage before committing your real burst.","whys":["At 1 he has only Timewinder on 9s and no Phase Dive yet, so the throw window is free","Same at 2 — the shield is on Parallel Convergence and needs him to walk into it","Your dash and burst still beat his kit cleanly since Chronobreak isn't up yet or is easily baited","Your full string kills him outright as long as Chronobreak is still on cooldown","Item lead widens your burst advantage faster than his slower Riftmaker timing allows him to scale","Snowballing denies him the farm and safety needed to reach his power spike","Once he has Riftmaker sustain plus a ready Chronobreak, he can tank and rewind through your combo"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"fizz","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Fizz","Fizz","Fizz"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Fizz needs Urchin Strike (550 range, 8s at rank 1) and Seastone Trident procs to threaten you — bait the hop with one spell, then W in while that short dash is still down."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Playful/Trickster (R) makes him untargetable and immune to your burst mid-combo, so if he times it right your R-E-Q-W whiffs entirely and he gets a free counter-poke."},{"when":"1st item","text":"His first item (usually Riftmaker or Lich Bane) plus Trickster uptime lets him dive onto you and dodge your burst window consistently — this is where the lane starts slipping from you."},{"when":"Late","text":"With Playful down to 8s and full burst items, Fizz can hop in, dodge the combo entirely, and land Chum the Waters to hold you there while he deletes you."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait his Urchin Strike dash before committing your own engage","Fight him only when Playful/Trickster is confirmed down","Snowball the first levels hard, before Chum the Waters arrives at 6"],"foe":["Land Urchin Strike/Trident procs to poke you down safely","Save Playful/Trickster to dodge your burst combo on cooldown reads","Itemize burst (Riftmaker/Lich Bane) to punch through your HP before you land your own combo"]},"early":"Before level 3 Fizz has no way to dodge your engage at all — Urchin Strike is a 550-range dash on 8s, not an escape, so bait it out, W in right after, and the trade is clean every time. From 3 he has Playful on 16s, so spend one spell to draw the hop before the rest of the string. Snowball this window before Chum the Waters arrives at 6.","mid":"Playful/Trickster is the whole matchup, and it is his E — it makes him untargetable, dodges your entire burst, and lets him land a free counter-hit while you're mid-animation. If he holds it until you commit, your string whiffs and he punishes you back safely; at 6 he adds Chum the Waters, 120s, which grounds your return. Bait the hop with a half-commit or wait out its 8-to-16 seconds before going all-in.","late":"With a burst item online and Playful up, Fizz becomes one of the best duelists into squishy mages in the game — he can hop over your engage, dodge your combo, and one-shot you before you can react. Play from max range, never commit without a read on his 8s Playful, and shut him down early since he snowballs hard once he's ahead.","whys":["No escape tool pre-6 means every dash-in trade goes your way if you bait his short Urchin Strike dash first","Same through level 5 — his kit still can't answer your dash without the ultimate available","Playful arrives at level 3, but on 16s it cannot cover every step you take from 700 range away","Playful is 14s at rank 2 — spend one spell to draw the hop, then the string lands clean","Chum the Waters sticks to you and grounds the return, so the shark knocks you into his whole combo","Snowballed Fizz becomes one of the best 1v1 duelists in the game against squishy burst mages","Fully scaled, Playful dodges your engage and Chum the Waters deletes you before you can react"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"galio","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Galio's Justice Punch is a knockup dash on 11s at rank 1, and Winds of War is another 11s — W in while both are down and take the trade, then leave before Shield of Durand can taunt you at 275 range."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Hero's Entrance is 180s at rank 1 and lands on an ally, so his 6 is a roam tool and not a lane threat — punish the wave he shoves, and track that 180s before you follow him."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Luden's burst outpaces his tank-support itemization completely; he simply doesn't build enough damage to threaten your combo at any point in this matchup."},{"when":"Late","text":"Even with Zhonya's and a taunt (W) online, Galio has no way to burst you down — he's a peel/engage tool for his team, so late game you just need to bait his taunt before committing your own all-in, and you still win the 1v1 handily."}],"wants":{"you":["Punish every wave push before he hits 6","Play around and bait his roam ultimate rather than his lane presence","Snowball hard since he has almost no lane pressure to punish your aggression"],"foe":["Farm safely with W taunt as a zoning tool","Look for a Hero's Entrance roam to save a bot lane fight","Build tank items and hope to survive teamfights rather than win lane"]},"early":"Galio is softer in lane than his reputation — Justice Punch is a knockup dash on 11s from level 3, Winds of War is another 11s, and Shield of Durand only taunts inside 275 range. Chunk him every time he farms and commit the moment his E or the taunt is spent; his own damage cannot take a bar before your return timer expires.","mid":"Hero's Entrance is a team tool — a shield and a landing on an ally, 180s at rank 1 — so his 6 changes nothing in lane, and your full string still chunks him hard whenever he is caught pushed up. Keep shoving and roam freely; he has no wave clear fast enough to punish you leaving. Track that 180s and leave when it does, or you arrive into a collapse he set up.","late":"Even fully itemized as a tank, Galio brings taunt and a shield to teamfights but essentially zero personal kill pressure — bait his W taunt with a fake engage, then burst him or dive past him onto the real threats. You continue to win any 1v1 against him at every stage of the game; the only risk is his taunt locking you down mid-combo against his team.","whys":["No dash and a slow telegraphed Q means every trade before level 3 goes your way for free","Same at 2 — Shield of Durand is his W, and the taunt on it only reaches enemies within 275 range","Justice Punch arrives at 3 on 11s — a knockup dash, so poke on its downtime and not into it","Winds of War is 11s at rank 1 and Justice Punch another 11s — poke in the gap between them","Hero's Entrance is 180s and lands elsewhere — his 6 adds nothing that stops your string","Snowballing is trivial since he brings no scaling damage threat to punish your aggression","Even full-build his taunt is the only tool he has against you, and your burst still wins the trade"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"hwei","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Once you have all three spells you can chain Ethereal Chains, Sigil of Malice and the Distortion detonation before Hwei lands Devastating Fire off his Subject: Disaster palette; every paint of his is a two-step cast while you threaten instant burst."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Your Mimic lets you double-cast Sigil of Malice through the Subject: Serenity shield if he mistimes it, and Spiraling Despair — 120s, and it has to strike you first — is too slow to punish the dash-in dash-out pattern."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Lost Chapter into Luden's gives you enough one-rotation burst to delete Hwei from range before his own combo (which needs multiple casts to fully resolve) finishes ticking."},{"when":"Late","text":"Hwei's teamfight paintings and long-range poke matter more in 5v5s, but in the actual lane duel he still can't out-trade your reset combo, so you keep priority into every mid-game roam window."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait Devastating Fire off his Disaster palette before you go in","Punish any moment his Subject: Serenity shield is down","Use Mimic to reset burst after he blows a cooldown"],"foe":["Land a Disaster paint into Crushing Maw to stop you ever closing","Hold Subject: Serenity as a reactive shield against your engage","Poke with long-range Disaster paints without overextending"]},"early":"Levels 1-2 you both poke at max range with skill shots, but LeBlanc's W lets you threaten a fast all-in that Hwei's slow cast times can't answer in kind, so you win most trades if you force the issue.","mid":"From level 3 through 6 you get full access to the W-E-Q chain burst combo and Hwei is still stitching together multi-part paintings — dodge his first cast, close the gap, and your root-into-burst just outpaces anything he can answer with, and your level 6 Mimic reset combo is a clean kill threat he can't match.","late":"Once Luden's is up you one-rotation him from range before his own combo finishes resolving, and even though his ultimate adds teamfight utility later, in the 1v1 he's still stuck reacting to your engage timing rather than dictating it.","whys":["Level 1 favours LeBlanc because her poke trades and W setup punish Hwei's slower cast times before he has his full kit online","Level 2 stays LeBlanc-favoured as she gains E to threaten a real all-in Hwei can't yet answer","Level 3 is LeBlanc-favoured once the full W-E-Q burst chain comes online well before Hwei can land a complete combo","Levels 4-5 remain LeBlanc-favoured as burst damage scaling outpaces Hwei's incremental poke","Level 6 favours LeBlanc: the Mimic double-burst beats Spiraling Despair, a 120s vision he must land first","First item favours LeBlanc because a Luden's-powered rotation kills him before his signature ever completes","At two items his paints out-range you in a teamfight, but the 1v1 still belongs to your reset burst"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"irelia","win":["Irelia","Irelia","Irelia","Irelia","Skill","Skill","Irelia"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Irelia threatens you the moment Flawless Duet (E) Marks you — Bladesurge refreshes on Marked targets, so one stun becomes two dashes. Poke only while her E is on its 16s."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Mimic doubles your chain, but her level 6 is Vanguard's Edge: 950 range, Marks you, and drops a wall of blades that slows you out of the W return window."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Stormsurge or Shadowflame burst can delete her, but Bladesurge heals on every dash and Ionian Fervor stacks her attack speed — buy Grievous Wounds first."},{"when":"Late","text":"Even fed, Irelia is a melee skirmisher who has to walk into your max-range poke and burst to do anything, so she leans on roams and teamfight flanks rather than winning the direct lane."}],"wants":{"you":["Poke her minion-reset windows so she can't Q off a wave","Bait a dash before landing E (Ethereal Chains) at max range","Punish her once all-in with instant W-E-Q burst"],"foe":["Land Flawless Duet to Mark you and chain Bladesurge resets in","Wait for a jungler gank to force the trade","Use minions as Q resets to close the gap safely"]},"early":"Levels 1-2 Bladesurge dashes 600 units off any minion for 15 mana, so Irelia reaches you whenever she wants — poke from max range and step back before her auto lands.","mid":"From level 3 her Flawless Duet (E) is the whole matchup: it stuns, Marks you, and refunds Bladesurge, so a single 16s cooldown decides every trade. Dodge the converge and your Q-W-E chain punishes her on the way out.","late":"Items don't change the core problem — she's still a melee champion who must close distance into your longest-range kit in the game, so she's forced to look for roams and flank angles in fights rather than winning lane 1v1.","whys":["Level 1 favours Irelia — Bladesurge (Q) dashes off any minion and her autos beat your 700-range Q","Level 2 stays LeBlanc-favoured since Irelia still has no reliable gap closer without minion stacks","Level 3 favours Irelia: Flawless Duet (E) stuns at 850 range and Marked targets refresh Bladesurge (Q) instantly","Levels 4-5 stay Irelia's: her E drops to 11.5s while your W return is your only exit — poke, never linger","Level 6 is skill: Mimic doubles your chain, but Vanguard's Edge Marks you and its wall slows your escape route","First item is skill: Stormsurge burst can delete her, but Bladesurge heals each dash — bring anti-heal","2+ items favours Irelia — she survives one chain and her 85s Vanguard's Edge wins fights you must skip"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"karma","win":["Karma","Karma","Skill","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"The root is Focused Resolve (W), a 675-range tether — leave that range before it completes and it never fires. Mantra Q is a delayed zone you walk out of, so nothing here holds you still."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Your Mimic on a 45s cooldown threatens a full kill in one rotation. Karma has no level 6 spike at all — Mantra needs no skill point and has been up since level 1, so her sixth level only shortens it to 38 seconds."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Stormsurge burst punishes her utility kit, but Mantra W heals her through the tether — one Grievous Wounds item makes extended trades one-sided."},{"when":"Late","text":"Karma becomes a pure enabler with RW shields and RE roams for her team, but she still can't win the 1v1 against your burst, so she's forced to play passive and use mantra defensively just to survive poke."}],"wants":{"you":["Force her to burn Mantra defensively before all-inning","Punish her when Focused Resolve (W) breaks","Roam off her lack of kill pressure once she's pushed"],"foe":["Hold Mantra for a defensive RW shield against your burst","Poke safely with base Q (Inner Flame) without overextending","Look for RE (Inspire) roams since she can't win lane trades"]},"early":"Levels 1-2 Karma's base Q poke stings but she has no kill pressure, so you can farm through it and look to punish any overextension with your own trades.","mid":"From level 3 the threat is Focused Resolve (W): a 675-range tether that roots only if you stay inside it, on a flat 12 seconds. Break it and she is a poke mage with a shield. Bait Inspire, wait out the 12, then Q-W-RW — her level 6 adds no burst window the way yours does.","late":"She shifts into a full support-mage role for her team with RW shields and RE roam speed, but in the direct 1v1 she still can't out-damage your combo, so she's stuck playing reactive and mantra-defensive rather than contesting you.","whys":["Level 1 favours Karma — Mantra needs no skill point, so her 950-range Inner Flame outranges your 700 Q","Level 2 stays Karma's: Focused Resolve tethers at 675 on a flat 12s and roots you unless you break it","Level 3 is skill: the root is Focused Resolve (W), a tether — leave its 675 range and it never lands","Levels 4-5 remain LeBlanc-favoured since Karma's damage scaling never catches up to LeBlanc's burst","Level 6 favours LeBlanc as Mimic reset burst is a real kill threat while Karma's ultimate unlock adds no new damage spike","First item favours LeBlanc because Luden's burst punishes Karma's inherently low base damage kit","2+ items favours LeBlanc as Karma is forced into a pure enabler role rather than contesting the lane"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"kassadin","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Skill","Skill","Kassadin","Kassadin"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Before level 6 Kassadin has no escape, but Null Sphere shields magic on a 9s cooldown and Void Stone cuts your damage — force the orb out, then run the full Q-W-E chain."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Riftwalk is a 5s, 500-range blink for 40 mana, and repeat casts cost escalating mana — his escape is real but finite, so drain the bar with wave pressure before you commit your chain."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Your Stormsurge spike is the last clean kill window: Void Stone's magic reduction plus his first big item means every later burst arrives blunted, so cash the lead now."},{"when":"Late","text":"This is the one phase where Kassadin's Riftwalk spam and magic resist stacking make him a genuine anti-mage threat in teamfights, but by then you should already have banked a lane lead."}],"wants":{"you":["Punish every last-hit attempt before level 6 with poke and all-ins","Snowball a lead hard enough that his late-game scaling doesn't matter","Track his mana before committing to a longer trade"],"foe":["Survive to level 6 and beyond with minimal deaths","Farm safely under tower and avoid all-ins pre-6","Stack magic resist and mana items to scale into late game"]},"early":"Levels 1-2 Kassadin farms with Nether Blade, a 7-second auto reset that restores mana, so chip damage does not starve him — poke only when Null Sphere is down.","mid":"Levels 3 to 5 are your window, but every spell you cast near him charges Force Pulse, so poke with E at 925 and stay out of its 400-range cone. At 6, Riftwalk is a 5-second blink for 40 mana — you no longer get to pick the moment, so open on the orb and chain him only when his mana is low.","late":"Kassadin's real strength shows up only once he's stacked magic resist and can spam Riftwalk in extended fights, which is a late-game teamfight identity — in the lane and in skirmishes before that point you should already be ahead, so the matchup is about banking your early advantage before his scaling catches up.","whys":["Level 1 favours LeBlanc as Kassadin is mana-starved with zero kill pressure this early","Level 2 stays LeBlanc-favoured for the same reason, plus he still lacks any engage tool","Level 3 favours LeBlanc since her burst chain punishes his continued passivity while waiting for level 6","Levels 4-5 are skill: every spell you cast near him charges Force Pulse, so poke buys him his cone slow","Level 6 is skill: Riftwalk is 5s at rank 1, so he leaves your chain — but Null Sphere still eats your burst","First item favours Kassadin: Void Stone cuts your magic damage and Rod of Ages ends your kill window","2+ items favours Kassadin — Riftwalk drops to 2 seconds and Void Stone plus MR stacking makes your burst a nuisance, not a kill"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"katarina","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Katarina","LeBlanc","Katarina"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"At level 3 Shunpo (E) blinks 725 to a target or to a dagger from Bouncing Blade (Q) or Preparation (W), so the daggers on the ground are her real cooldown — poke from behind the wave and she has nothing to jump to."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Death Lotus is a 75-second channel at 550 range, and Voracity slashes her cooldowns whenever someone she damaged dies. It is her strongest lane window — but it is a channel, so Ethereal Chains or a mimicked chain shuts it off mid-cast."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Your burst spike from Luden's or Shadowflame lets you punish her positioning again before her own first item (often Rocketbelt for extra Shunpo range) fully comes online."},{"when":"Late","text":"With multiple resets Katarina can bounce off wave and jungler daggers to chain kills across the map, but your reset combo and higher single-target burst still win a clean 1v1 if she doesn't already have reset fodder nearby."}],"wants":{"you":["Avoid standing near daggers she can Shunpo off of","Punish her before level 6 unlocks the Death Lotus channel","Bait Shunpo before committing your own burst"],"foe":["Bait your Distortion (W) before dashing in with Shunpo","Farm daggers safely to build reset chains","Look for all-in windows right at level 6"]},"early":"Levels 1-2 Katarina has no reset engine yet, so your ranged poke and burst threat keep her passive and farming carefully under pressure.","mid":"Levels 3-5 stay yours: your E outranges Shunpo by 200 and she has to walk into 700-range Q. Level 6 flips it — Death Lotus is 550-range and Voracity refunds her cooldowns off any death nearby, so hold Ethereal Chains for the channel rather than spending it on the wave.","late":"With items she can chain Shunpo resets across multiple daggers for map-wide kill threats, and her burst-into-reset pattern means a single bad trade can snowball into her ultimate again, so you need a clean lane lead or peel before engaging her late.","whys":["Level 1 favours LeBlanc as Katarina has no engage or reset tools to punish poke this early","Level 2 stays LeBlanc-favoured: Preparation is a 15s speed boost, not an escape","Level 3 favours LeBlanc — Shunpo arrives at 725 range on 12s, and your E outranges it by 200","Levels 4-5 remain LeBlanc-favoured: deny the daggers and Shunpo has nothing to blink to but you","Level 6 favours Katarina: Death Lotus is 75s, and Voracity slashes her cooldowns to nothing the moment anyone dies","First item favours LeBlanc — Q-W-RW kills her inside the 550-range Death Lotus, and your E cancels the channel outright","2+ items favours Katarina since reset chains off multiple daggers give her repeatable kill windows late"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"lissandra","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Lissandra"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Lissandra needs Ice Shard (Q) at 725 to slow you into Ring of Frost (W), which roots at just 450 — stay outside that circle and her whole setup is a slow with no follow-up, while your E reaches 925."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Her level 6 is Frozen Tomb (R), not Glacial Path (E): cast on you it stuns, cast on herself it heals her and makes her untargetable and invulnerable — so never dump Mimic into the ice, wait it out."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Your Stormsurge spike arrives before her damage items matter, but Frozen Tomb heals her — Grievous Wounds decides whether the burst finishes."},{"when":"Late","text":"Lissandra's Frozen Tomb ultimate becomes a genuine teamfight-turning peel/engage tool with full CDR, so while she can't win the lane 1v1, she becomes a much scarier utility threat in 5v5s than she ever was in the direct duel."}],"wants":{"you":["Punish her lack of gap-closer before level 6","Dodge Ice Shard so its slow never sets up her root","Burst her down before she can loop Glacial Path back to safety"],"foe":["Land Q into W stun combo to start damage trades","Use Glacial Path to re-engage or disengage safely","Wait for full build before contesting extended fights"]},"early":"Levels 1-2 Lissandra has no reliable way to close distance or punish you at range, so your poke wins these trades cleanly while she's stuck setting up slow root combos.","mid":"Levels 3 to 6 her damage needs Ice Shard's slow to land Ring of Frost inside 450, so chain her from 925 and she never gets the root. Glacial Path is her E and it is 24 seconds at rank 1 — while it is down she cannot leave the wave you are shoving into her.","late":"Once she has CDR and full build, Frozen Tomb becomes a serious teamfight-defining ultimate for locking down carries or peeling for her own team, and that's where Lissandra actually shines — but in isolated lane trades she still can't out-duel your burst.","whys":["Level 1 favours LeBlanc as Lissandra has zero gap-closing tools to punish poke this early","Level 2 stays LeBlanc-favoured since she's still purely reliant on slow root setups","Level 3 favours LeBlanc because her burst chain lands before Lissandra's Q-W combo fully resolves","Levels 4-5 remain LeBlanc-favoured as burst scaling keeps outpacing her root-reliant damage","Level 6 favours LeBlanc — Frozen Tomb is 120s, so once she self-casts it she has no answer for two minutes","First item favours LeBlanc as her burst spike arrives before Lissandra's own damage items come online","2+ items favours Lissandra because full-build Frozen Tomb becomes a genuine teamfight-winning ultimate outside the 1v1"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"locke","win":["Skill","Skill","Skill","Skill","Locke","Skill","Locke"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Both champions have full combos online by level 3-5 and neither has an inherent statistical edge yet, so trades come down to who lands their skill shots and dodges the other's burst setup first."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Locke's ultimate gives a real spike that lets a well-timed all-in punish LeBlanc harder than she can answer if she's already used Distortion to reposition, so this is Locke's best window in the lane."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Whoever completes their burst item first typically dictates the next few trades, so this phase is purely a function of who farmed cleaner and itemized first, not an inherent kit advantage."},{"when":"Late","text":"Locke's late-game kit scales into a stronger all-in and disengage-punish tool than LeBlanc's reset combo can fully answer, giving Locke the edge in extended late-game skirmishes."}],"wants":{"you":["Play around your own cooldowns rather than assume a kit edge","Use Mimic and Distortion to escape Locke's level 6 all-in window","Contest trades only when you have full combo up"],"foe":["Farm safely into your item and level 6 spikes","Look for the level 6 all-in window against LeBlanc's repositioning","Punish LeBlanc late once your kit scaling outpaces her reset combo"]},"early":"Levels 1-2 there's no clear statistical favourite between LeBlanc and Locke, so this window comes down to who executes trades and last-hitting more cleanly rather than any kit mismatch.","mid":"Levels 3-5 remain an even skill matchup with both combos online, but level 6 tips toward Locke since their ultimate creates a genuine all-in spike that punishes LeBlanc if she's already spent Distortion to reposition.","late":"First item is again a toss-up decided by who itemizes and farms better, but by the late game Locke's kit scaling gives them the edge in extended trades, since LeBlanc's reset combo alone isn't enough to answer Locke's stronger late-game all-in and disengage-punish potential.","whys":["Level 1 is Skill/even as neither champion holds an inherent kit advantage this early","Level 2 stays Skill/even for the same reason, trades depend on execution not kit","Level 3 is Skill/even since both full combos are online with no clear statistical edge","Levels 4-5 remain Skill/even as the matchup is decided by play, not a kit mismatch","Level 6 favours Locke because their ultimate creates a genuine all-in spike that outpaces LeBlanc's repositioning tools","First item is Skill/even since it's decided by who farms and itemizes cleaner, not an inherent advantage","2+ items favours Locke as their late-game scaling edges out LeBlanc's reset combo in extended late fights"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"lux","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Once Q-W-E are online, Sigil dash into Distortion snap-back gives you a burst window Light Binding can't punish if you bait the root with the wraith clone first."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Chain W-Q-R after baiting Light Binding or Lucent Singularity and you delete her from a range she thinks is safe; Final Spark takes 60 seconds to come back."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Lost Chapter/Shadowflame timing lets you clear waves faster and threaten roams while she's stuck farming with a pure skillshot kit and no escape."},{"when":"Late","text":"Even with a completed Luden's, Lux is a stationary artillery piece with no dash — your mobility and burst still let you jump her before her shield or root land."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait Light Binding with the clone before committing R","Punish her mana and cooldowns early — she has no sustain past 3-4 spells","Roam once the wave is pushed; she has zero kill pressure to punish a shove"],"foe":["Land Light Binding pre-6 to threaten a combo","Hold Final Spark for exactly when she can capitalize, not before","Play for late-game teamfight AOE since 1v1 is lost"]},"early":"Lux's only real threat pre-6 is landing Light Binding into Lucent Singularity, and if either misses she has nothing — you can walk up in melee range behind minions and punish every whiffed skillshot with Sigil poke. Your clone lets you feint an all-in, forcing her to waste Binding on the fake, after which you dash in for free. She out-ranges you on paper but her projectiles are slow and telegraphed, so with decent dodge timing you win nearly every trade from level 2 onward. Shove hard and threaten her under tower, since she has no escape if you commit while her cooldowns are down.","mid":"At 6 your full combo (W-Q-Sigil-R) one-shots her from nearly 700 range if she is caught without Light Binding up, and her Final Spark reaches 3340 but wants the root to land first — something you never allow. Bait the root with the clone, then all-in the real body the instant it is spent; Light Binding is a flat 10 seconds at every rank, and that is a long window to roam or shove in. Watch her autos too: Illumination charges on every spell she lands and her next attack detonates it, so the auto is the second half of her damage.","late":"Lux scales into a real teamfight threat with Luden's and Final Spark poke, but she's still a stationary, no-mobility mage who dies instantly to a single R-flash dive from you or your jungler. Your job late is picking her off in the fog before she can set up Binding chains on your backline; she cannot answer an assassin who chooses the engage. As long as you don't walk face-first into a pre-aimed ultimate, this matchup stays your favor all the way to 40 minutes.","whys":["Lux has no engage or escape at level 1, so your poke and clone-bait trades win most exchanges","Same lack of mobility keeps her losing short trades as your kit scales faster per point","Landing Light Binding is her only lifeline, and at a flat 10s it cannot flip your all-in","With Sigil-Distortion online you can burst through her shield before Singularity detonates","Your R combo one-shots her from range her Final Spark can't reliably punish first","Lost Chapter/core item spike widens your roam and wave-clear advantage over her","Even fully itemized she has zero mobility to dodge your engage in the late game"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"malzahar","win":["Skill","Skill","Skill","Skill","Malzahar","Skill","Malzahar"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Malefic Visions poke plus Void Swarm minions punishes your melee-range Sigil trades, so early exchanges are closer than the late-game numbers suggest."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Nether Grasp is a 140-second suppression at 700 range, and Call of the Void silences rather than roots — if the silence lands first your W return is gone and the suppress finishes you."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Once you have Shadowflame/Sigil online you out-damage his DOT in a straight burst race, provided you dodge Call of the Void — his only real threat."},{"when":"Late","text":"Malzahar's ult still deletes squishies in teamfights, but 1v1 you snowball harder off kills and roams than he ever will off passive Visions damage."}],"wants":{"you":["Stay out of Call of the Void's straight-line skillshot range when pushed in","Look to roam past level 6 since he has no mobility to punish your leave","Force all-ins only after baiting the Call of the Void silence"],"foe":["Land Call of the Void to set up his suppress combo","Farm safely with Visions poke and avoid short trades","Group early since he offers strong teamfight suppress but weak 1v1 tempo"]},"early":"Malzahar's Void Swarm minions and Malefic Visions make early trading annoying, since Visions ticks for 11 seconds and refreshes whenever he casts anything else on you. Call of the Void is a 900-range skillshot that silences — not roots — so it does not hold you, it takes your W return away. Void Shift also gives him damage reduction and CC immunity until he has taken damage, so chip first, then commit. His Q is only 6 seconds, so a whiff is a short window, not a free lane.","mid":"Level 6 is where Malzahar flips the lane: Nether Grasp is a 700-range suppression on 140 seconds at rank 1, and if Call of the Void silences you first, Distortion's return is unavailable and you die where you landed. Never all-in him blind past 6 — bait the silence with the clone, then commit once it is spent. But Nether Grasp is a channel: Ethereal Chains, or a mimicked chain, breaks it outright, which is the one answer his kit has no reply to. Hold E for that, not for the wave.","late":"Late game Malzahar becomes a suppress-and-poke teamfight piece with real threat if his ult catches your backline, but 1v1 and in skirmishes you still burst him down faster than his Visions dot ticks. Play around not getting isolated when his ult is up, and otherwise keep pressuring side lanes and picking him off in the fog — his lack of any dash or blink means once you're on him, he's dead.","whys":["No engage or escape for Malzahar at level 1 lets your trades and clone-bait win the lane","Level 2 is skill: Malefic Visions ticks on 11s and refreshes off his other spells","Level 3 is skill: Call of the Void silences, so it deletes your W return — but it is a 900-range shot you can dodge","Levels 4-5 are skill: his Q is a flat 6s, so the dodge-then-commit window is short","Level 6 favours Malzahar — Nether Grasp suppresses at 700 range on a 140s cooldown at rank 1","First item is skill: your burst spike arrives, but Nether Grasp is back every 110 seconds by then","2+ items favours Malzahar: an 80-second suppression removes you from any fight you tried to start"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"mel","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Mel"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Mel's Radiant Volley poke is real, but Solar Snare (E) on 11s is her only lockdown — dodge it and you win the all-in window once Sigil-Distortion is online."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Your mimicked combo kills her once Rebuttal ends — chains and sigils fired into that barrier reflect back at you, so bait the 26-second W with the clone first."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Shadowflame timing edges out her Luden's-style scaling since you're the one deciding when fights start."},{"when":"Late","text":"Golden Eclipse hits every target carrying Overwhelm stacks anywhere on the map, so chip damage she landed minutes ago becomes an execute — stay above the threshold."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait Rebuttal's barrier with the clone so your real burst lands clean","Punish her lack of dash/blink early and mid — she has no true escape until items","Close the game before Golden Eclipse's global execute makes picks riskier for you"],"foe":["Play patiently and farm through early aggression, since she has no engage of her own","Time Rebuttal to reflect your chain and punish your all-in commitment","Scale into items where global Golden Eclipse and Rebuttal carry teamfights"]},"early":"Mel pokes with Radiant Volley on a 10-second cooldown and has no dash or blink to escape you, but Solar Snare (E) roots at 1000 range every 11 seconds and is the shot you must dodge. Her barrier is Rebuttal (W), not her ultimate: it blanks her damage taken and reflects your Ethereal Chains and sigil straight back, so force it with the Mirror Image before your real body commits. Between Rebuttal windows she cannot leave, and you win short trades from level 3 onward.","mid":"By level 6 your full combo (W-Q-Sigil-R) kills her cleanly as long as you don't dump it into a raised Rebuttal — the barrier blanks the damage and throws your projectiles back, so force it with a fake engage first and commit inside its 26-second downtime. She has no dash or blink to leave once the chain lands, and her ranged damage can't out-trade your burst in a straight 1v1. Keep roaming and pressuring; with no mobility she cannot punish your rotations.","late":"Late game Mel becomes a genuine threat: Golden Eclipse is global and strikes everyone carrying Overwhelm stacks, scaling with the chip damage she has already put on you, and Rebuttal punishes messy engages from squishy assassins like you. This is the one phase where the numbers flip toward her. Close the game before it arrives, and if you must fight her late, bait Rebuttal down first every time and keep your health clear of the execute threshold.","whys":["Mel lacks any mobility or hard engage at level 1, so your clone-bait trades win the lane cleanly","Her poke alone can't stop your combo pressure as your kit comes online through these levels","Landing your chain outside Rebuttal keeps this phase clearly in your favor — dodge Solar Snare","Shadowflame timing lets you dictate fight timing before her scaling items complete","Bait-then-burst still kills her before her own damage output catches up in a straight fight","Item power spike still favors your proactive playstyle over her more reactive kit","Golden Eclipse's global Overwhelm damage and Rebuttal finally win her the late skirmishes"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"naafiri","win":["Naafiri","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Naafiri","Naafiri","Naafiri"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Naafiri's dash-heavy all-ins hit hard early, but Eviscerate is 11s at rank 1 — bait it with your clone and burst her while the dash and the pack heal are both down."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Your R combo one-shots her from range her Eviscerate dash can't close fast enough to punish, as long as you don't stand still after landing Sigil."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Sigil/Shadowflame lets you out-trade her burst window entirely, since her damage falls off hard if she can't reset off a kill."},{"when":"Late","text":"Naafiri dives backlines with untargetable frames on W, E and R — burst timed into any of them is wasted, so hold the chain until she has already landed on you."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait her all-in dash with the clone before committing your real burst","Buy Grievous Wounds early — Eviscerate fully heals the pack every 7 seconds","Avoid standing in melee range once her Q/E combo is up"],"foe":["Look for all-in windows when packmates are up and you're overextended","Land Hounds' Pursuit to start dives she does not have to aim","Dive with dashes to close the gap on your ranged poke"]},"early":"Naafiri is an early bully whose Darkin Daggers reach 900 range, out-ranging your Sigil's 700, and the second dagger hits harder once the bleed is on you. Never trade in melee range on cooldown — bait Eviscerate with the clone, let her spend the dash into the fake, and punish with your real body while it sits on its 11-second cooldown. Levels 2 to 5 are your window: the Q-W double proc chunks her, and her packmates take your W damage on the way through the wave.","mid":"At 6 your full combo can kill her, but Hounds' Pursuit locks on from 900 range and its recast grants a shield, so she picks the moment, not you. The Call of the Pack — 26s at rank 1, 18s at rank 5 — turns her untargetable mid-commit and blanks a mimicked burst outright, so wait it out rather than dumping into it. Ward river, respect her all-in whenever her jungler is near, and take the fight only while Eviscerate is down; with her dashes spent the kit really does fall apart.","late":"Naafiri stays dangerous as a dive assassin, and Hounds' Pursuit is a 900-range lock-on she never has to aim, so playing at range is not an answer by itself. Use the clone to scout, hold Ethereal Chains for the moment she lands instead of spending it early, and keep Grievous Wounds on her so Eviscerate's full pack heal cannot carry the extended fight.","whys":["Darkin Daggers pokes from 900 range with bleed and the pack chases the mark you cannot answer","Same pattern continues as her damage falls off once her mobility is spent and yours isn't","Her all-in combo can genuinely punish you if caught, but disciplined spacing keeps this in your favor overall","Sigil/Shadowflame timing lets your ranged poke outpace her need to close distance","Hounds' Pursuit locks on from 900 range and recasts into a shield, so your burst window closes","Her first lethality item plus Eviscerate's pack heal wins the extended trades you cannot refuse","Untargetable frames on W, E and R blank the timed burst your late game depends on"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"neeko","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Neeko"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Tangle-Barbs (E) is the root, not Blooming Burst, and it passes straight through minions on a 12-second cooldown — bait it with the clone first."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Your R combo kills her before Pop Blossom's leap lands, but its preparation is hidden while she is disguised — read the health bar, not the model."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Sigil/Shadowflame lets you snowball trades hard, since Neeko's damage output can't match your burst speed 1v1."},{"when":"Late","text":"Neeko's disguise and ult make her a real teamfight/pick threat with Pop Blossom stun chains, flipping the matchup toward her in group skirmishes."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait her root with the clone, not your real body, before all-in windows","Snowball early trades hard since her scaling comes from teamfight utility, not 1v1 power","Avoid grouping blind late — her disguise can set up ambushes with allies"],"foe":["Land Tangle-Barbs to enable a real combo or peel","Play around disguise for pick setups with jungle or support","Group for Pop Blossom stun-chain teamfight potential late"]},"early":"Neeko's kit revolves around landing Tangle-Barbs (E) — Blooming Burst (Q) is the seed poke, the tangle is the root, and it passes through the whole wave on a 12-second cooldown, so standing behind minions saves you from nothing. Sidestep it, or eat it on the Mirror Image, since she has no dash or blink to save herself once it is spent, then all-in with your real body. Play around that one cooldown and you win almost every early skirmish.","mid":"At 6 your full combo still kills her outright before Pop Blossom lands, provided you open from outside the 1000-range tangle and don't let her set up first. The ultimate knocks up on the leap and stuns on the landing inside 600 range, so in a straight 1v1 it does little for her unless the root goes in first. Keep pressuring and roaming, but respect the disguise on rotations: Inherent Glamour hides the Pop Blossom preparation, and the ally you walk past may be her.","late":"Neeko becomes a real threat in group skirmishes once her Pop Blossom can chain-stun your team and her disguise lets her set up surprise picks alongside allies. This is where the matchup shifts — her value comes from teamfight utility rather than 1v1 power, so avoid isolated grouping without vision and don't underestimate a fake minion or ally near objectives. Play around this late-game utility spike rather than assuming your early lane dominance still applies in 5v5s.","whys":["Neeko's only punish tool at level 1 is her root, and baiting it with the clone wins you the trade","Her lack of any escape keeps you ahead as your combo damage scales up through these levels","Landing your full burst before the Pop Blossom leap keeps this phase clearly in your favor","Sigil/Shadowflame item spike widens your snowball since her 1v1 damage doesn't scale as fast","Your combo still kills her outright as long as you dictate engage range and avoid her root","Continued item advantage keeps your proactive tempo ahead of her more reactive kit","Pop Blossom's stun-chain and disguise setups finally give her real leverage in group fights"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"orianna","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Orianna"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Orianna's Command: Attack poke drops to a 3-second cooldown, but her shield is Command: Protect (E) on 9s — clone-bait that one, not Dissonance, and trades stay yours."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Your R combo kills her before Command: Shockwave can pull off a big teamfight play, provided you burst her before she can position the ball defensively."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Sigil/Shadowflame timing lets you snowball ahead of her scaling since her damage output needs the ball set up, which you can deny by pressuring her ball position."},{"when":"Late","text":"Orianna's Shockwave becomes a genuine teamfight-flipping ultimate with real pick and peel potential, tilting group skirmishes back toward her side."}],"wants":{"you":["Deny her the ball so Command: Protect cannot shield the target you want dead","Bait Command: Protect with the clone before committing your real all-in","Snowball early kills hard since her late-game utility outweighs her 1v1 power"],"foe":["Farm safely and use the ball defensively until core items complete","Look for Command: Shockwave setups with allies for pick potential","Group for Shockwave-into-follow-up combos in the late teamfight phase"]},"early":"Orianna's early kit is entirely built around slow, deliberate ball placement — she has no dash, no blink, and no way to escape once you've closed the distance. Bait Command: Protect (E) with the clone: the shield is on a flat 9 seconds and is her only defensive tool, so send the real body while it is down. Command: Dissonance (W) is the slow field, not a shield, and it is what stops your walk-up — cross it on the Distortion dash. Deny her the ball and most of her early lane presence goes with it.","mid":"At 6 your full combo still kills her outright before Command: Shockwave can be positioned for real value, as long as you burst quickly rather than giving her time to reposition the ball defensively. Her ultimate is a serious teamfight tool but does very little in an isolated 1v1 unless she's already set up favorably, which a well-timed clone-bait prevents. Keep roaming and applying pressure; she has no mobility to punish your rotations and her scaling really only matters once she has allies around her.","late":"Orianna's Command: Shockwave becomes a genuine game-defining ultimate in teamfights, capable of pulling off huge pick and peel plays that can flip a fight instantly. This is where the matchup shifts in her favor — her value is now tied to team utility rather than 1v1 power, so respect her positioning around objectives and avoid grouping into unfavorable Shockwave angles. Your early lane dominance doesn't fully carry into this phase, so play around her ball rather than assuming an easy dive.","whys":["Orianna has no engage or escape at level 1, letting your poke and clone-bait trades win consistently","Her slow ball setup continues to lose to your faster combo burst through these levels","Your full combo kills her before Shockwave can be meaningfully positioned in a 1v1","Sigil/Shadowflame spike widens your snowball as her scaling still needs teamfight setup to matter","Continued burst speed advantage keeps this phase in your favor if you deny her ball control","Item timing keeps your proactive playstyle ahead of her more passive scaling pattern","Command: Shockwave's teamfight-flipping potential finally tips group skirmishes toward her"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"pantheon","win":["Pantheon","Pantheon","Pantheon","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Pantheon's Shield Vault stun hits hard in short trades, but his lack of ranged poke means you can bait it with the clone and punish freely from range once it whiffs."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Your R combo still burst-kills him, but the ultimate is Grand Starfall — 5500 range on a 180-second timer — so ward the entries before you take a side lane."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Sigil/Shadowflame widens your damage lead since Pantheon's early all-in power doesn't scale linearly with items the way your burst does."},{"when":"Late","text":"Pantheon's Grand Starfall dive is still a threat for surprise picks on your team, but 1v1 he cannot answer your burst if you open from outside Shield Vault's 600 range."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait Shield Vault's stun with the clone before engaging for real","Poke from 700 pre-6 — Comet Spear only reaches 575 to answer","Ward for Grand Starfall dives once he hits 6, since the ult reaches 5500"],"foe":["Land Shield Vault to stun and combo before you can react","All-in hard early while his damage output is highest relative to yours","Use Grand Starfall for a surprise dive or teamfight engage later"]},"early":"Pantheon is a lane bully whose kit depends on landing Shield Vault's stun to open a full combo. His only ranged tool is Comet Spear at 575, so Sigil poke from 700 is the safe exchange. Bait the dash with the clone whenever he steps up, since he has no follow-up mobility once it is spent, then burst the real body while it sits on 13 seconds. Aegis Assault is a channel — Ethereal Chains cancels it outright — and its invulnerability only covers his front, so damage from behind still lands.","mid":"At 6 your full combo still kills him before Grand Starfall can land a follow-up dive, provided you keep vision up and don't get caught alone in unwarded areas. The ultimate is a 5500-range global on 180 seconds — frightening for surprise picks, but in a lane 1v1 it does nothing walking up would not, so the core interaction stands: bait Shield Vault, cancel Aegis Assault with the chain, then burst him. Keep roaming, since 575 range on Comet Spear cannot punish your rotations.","late":"Pantheon's Grand Starfall remains a real threat for surprise dives onto your backline or split-pushers, so ward the entry points and don't wander alone without vision. In a straight skirmish he answers burst with Aegis Assault, which covers his front for the whole channel, so open from behind or hold the chain to cancel it. Play around the dive threat positionally and this matchup stays in your favor late.","whys":["His Comet Spear and an empowered Mortal Will hit harder than anything you have at level 1","Shield Vault's stun on 13 seconds still turns any melee-range mistake into his kill","Aegis Assault blanks frontal burst, so his stun-into-shield pattern wins short trades","Sigil/Shadowflame widens your damage lead since his all-in power doesn't scale as cleanly with items","Continued burst advantage keeps this phase in your favor as long as you respect his stun timing","Item spike keeps your proactive engage options ahead of his shrinking all-in window","Global ult dive threat remains real late, but vision and spacing keep the 1v1 comfortably yours"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"qiyana","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Qiyana"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Once you have all three spells you can chain Sigil-E-Q from range before Qiyana closes the 650 on Audacity, and her Q only adds a slow or a stun when Terrashape has armed it."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Mimic doubles Chain into a near-instant kill on squishy Qiyana, and her engage is Audacity, not Elemental Wrath — root the dash."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Qiyana's first item all-in (with jungler) starts to actually threaten you if you're caught out of position near a river bush or wall."},{"when":"Late","text":"Late Qiyana one-shots you if she flanks off a wall, and her mobility with terrain resets makes her a nightmare in skirmishes if you're forced to group."}],"wants":{"you":["Poke with Q from max range before she gets in E-Q range","Deny wall proximity, especially river brush walls","Track jungler before committing all-in near level 6"],"foe":["Farm safely and wait for jungler gank near a wall","Use Terrashape off a wall to arm Q before she engages","Look for side lane skirmishes late where terrain resets let her reposition"]},"early":"Levels 1-2 you outrange her: Sigil reaches 700 and her Q only 525, so punish every step she takes toward a wall she wants Terrashape on. Play a poke lane and never let Audacity land on you first, because that dash is what starts her whole combo.","mid":"Levels 3-5 your full combo kills her from range before Terrashape into Audacity can close, so trade when she is away from walls — her Q is a flat 7 seconds rather than a charge system, and it only bites hard while the weapon is enchanted. At 6, Mimic-Chain deletes her, but hold R in reserve if her jungler shows for a coordinated dive.","late":"Once she has an item and roams with jungler pressure she becomes a real threat in skirmishes — her wall-resets let her reposition mid-fight and burst backline carries. Keep vision on river walls, don't walk in alone, and use your R to disengage rather than force fights once teams group.","whys":["You out-poke her at level 1 with Sigil range before she has any wall to combo off of","Audacity only reaches 650 and sits on 11 seconds at rank 1 before she can commit","Full combo online lets you burst her before she reaches E range","Levels 4-5 continue to favor your ranged all-in over her still-limited engage","Mimic-doubled Chain plus R disengage make you dominant right as she hits 6","Item power spikes still favor your ranged burst over her scaling all-in","Her wall-reset mobility and item-fueled one-shot potential finally make her the safer bet in late-game skirmishes"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"ryze","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Ryze"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Full combo LeBlanc punishes Ryze's short Q range and lack of any early burst — he can't trade back before you've already reset behind Sigil."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Mimic-doubled Chain plus Distortion snare secures kills on a champion with zero level-6 spike of his own."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Ryze needs multiple items and mana stacking before his combo does real damage, so this window is still yours to press."},{"when":"Late","text":"Full-build Ryze with Realm Warp and Flux-fed roots becomes nearly impossible to burst down or catch, and the portal moves his whole team 3000 units across the map."}],"wants":{"you":["Poke and all-in every level where he has no mana for Q spam","Deny CS with early aggression since he needs stacks to scale","Snowball your roams before he online-scales past you"],"foe":["Farm safely and avoid all trades until mana econ improves","Wait for a support gank or level 6 combo to punish overextension","Scale to 3+ items where the Flux-fed root chain becomes unavoidable"]},"early":"Levels 1-2 you already out-trade him — Ryze has almost no early kill pressure and burns mana fast, so poking him with Sigil-Q punishes every CS attempt. Keep pressing as he has no way to threaten you back before level 6.","mid":"Levels 3-6 your full burst combo kills him outright before his own combo does meaningful damage — he lacks the mana and item support to answer your all-in, and Mimic-Chain plus Distortion locks him down completely. This is your best window to snowball a lead or force him off farm.","late":"Once Ryze hits his mana-fuelled spikes with a couple of items, the Flux-into-Rune-Prison root is very hard to dodge and Realm Warp drops his team into a sidelane 3000 units away. Respect his 1000-range poke once he has scaled — your burst window has passed and you need picks or numbers now.","whys":["You dominate trades from minute one since Ryze has no early combo to punish your poke","Continued poke advantage as his mana pool still can't support consistent trading","Full combo online lets you kill him before his own damage output matters","Mid-game burst window still favors you heavily against his still-scaling kit","Mimic-Chain plus snare secures kills right as he reaches 6 with no answer","First-item Ryze still lacks the mana sustain to punish your aggression","Full-build Ryze scales into a real threat with his Flux-fed roots and Realm Warp map pressure"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"swain","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Swain"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Full combo LeBlanc bursts Swain down before Nevermove roots you — and it roots on the wave's return trip, not the throw, so step out as it comes back."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Mimic-doubled Chain punishes him hard, but Demonic Ascension turns a losing trade into a stall if Nevermove landed first — 120 seconds, so count it."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Ravenous Flock plus sustain items out-heals your poke in extended trades — buy Grievous Wounds at this spike and keep the windows short."},{"when":"Late","text":"Late Swain becomes an unkillable teamfight anchor — his ult heals through your burst entirely and drains a whole team, making him a nightmare to actually finish off."}],"wants":{"you":["Sidestep Nevermove's return, not just the outgoing wave","All-in fast in short windows before his healing outscales your burst","Roam early since he has weak early kill pressure without root"],"foe":["Land the Nevermove root to set up any trade or all-in","Farm safely under sustain until items and ult heal come online","Anchor teamfights late where his drain heals through burst"]},"early":"Levels 1-2 you control the lane — Swain has no way to punish you without landing his skillshot root first, so play around dodging it and poke him down with Sigil range. He's passive until he gets picks.","mid":"Levels 3-6 your full combo still kills him outright if you avoid his root, and Mimic-Chain plus a well-timed all-in denies him the setup he needs entirely. Watch for his ultimate stacking though — landing root into R can turn a bad fight for him into a stall.","late":"Once Swain has sustain items and his ultimate draining a full teamfight, he becomes extremely hard to actually kill — the healing outpaces your burst and he anchors fights from the backline. Focus picks before teamfights start, or burn him down before his ult channel completes.","whys":["You dominate early trades since Swain has zero pressure without landing his root first","Continued lane control as his kill threat still hinges entirely on hitting one skillshot","Full combo burst kills him before his own damage or healing can respond","Mid-game all-in windows still favor your burst over his root-dependent kit","Mimic-Chain burst remains lethal at 6 as long as you dodge his root setup","Sustain-item Swain starts surviving your combo through raw healing in extended trades","Full-build Swain's teamfight drain-ult heals through your burst and makes him unkillable late"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"sylas","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Sylas","LeBlanc","Sylas"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Full combo LeBlanc bursts Sylas before his own kit does anything meaningful, since his Q-E chain lacks the range to punish your poke first."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Sylas's Hijack steals your Mimic from 950 range — no E needed — and turns it back on you, so hold R for the exit and never reveal it while his 80-second ult is up."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Sylas's healing comes from Kingslayer (W), 9s at rank 3 — with early items it wins him extended trades even when your burst lands first."},{"when":"Late","text":"Full-build Sylas roams with a stolen ultimate and heals through your damage, making him a lane-and-teamfight nightmare that snowballs off any pick."}],"wants":{"you":["Burst him down fast in short trades before level 6 steals your ult","Avoid overextending into his E stun range once he's 6","Deny his roams by matching tempo and pushing waves"],"foe":["Farm safely and look to land E for a stun into all-in","Steal your Mimic with Hijack once you reveal your ultimate","Roam with a stolen ult to snowball other lanes post-6"]},"early":"Levels 1-2 you outrange and out-trade him easily — Sylas has no answer to Sigil poke before he has E stun range, so press every wave you can. He's forced passive until his kit comes online.","mid":"Levels 3-5 your combo still kills him first, but level 6 flips it: Hijack takes your Mimic from 950 range with no setup, so never show R while his ult is up. Abduct pulls, it does not stun — the danger is being dragged to 400 range where Kingslayer heals him through your combo.","late":"With sustain and a stolen ultimate, Sylas becomes a genuine threat who heals through your burst and roams to swing other lanes with your own stolen tools. Ward his roam paths, don't overextend, and try to burst him in isolated picks before teamfights where his healing and utility snowball out of control.","whys":["You dominate early trades with pure poke range before Sylas has any stun threat","Continued lane dominance as his kit still lacks the range to punish you","Full combo burst still kills him cleanly before level 6 changes the dynamic","Combo advantage holds through level 5 right up until his ultimate comes online","Hijack takes your Mimic from 950 range with no setup, flipping the level 6 spike","Kingslayer healing plus first-item power out-sustains a burst that does not kill","Full-build Sylas with a stolen ultimate and heal sustain snowballs the late-game hard against you"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"syndra","win":["Skill","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Syndra","LeBlanc","Syndra"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Full combo LeBlanc still bursts Syndra down fast if you dodge her Dark Sphere stun setup, since her own combo needs a landed sphere-stun to actually threaten you."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Syndra's Unleashed Power reaches huge burst range and can chain-stun into a kill on you first if she's the one landing spheres — respect her level 6 spike hard."},{"when":"1st item","text":"At first item her Unleashed Power is still a 120-second cooldown — commit in that gap, and play around sphere availability rather than raw stats."},{"when":"Late","text":"Late Syndra out-ranges and out-bursts you from farther away with fully stacked spheres, making her the safer bet in poke wars and pick comps."}],"wants":{"you":["Dodge her Dark Sphere placements to deny her stun setup","Punish her when she's on cooldown or missing spheres nearby","Burst fast in short windows before level 6 flips the range advantage"],"foe":["Stack spheres near you to threaten a stun-into-burst combo","Play around Unleashed Power range advantage at level 6","Poke from max range late where her scaling burst outranges yours"]},"early":"Level 1 is a coinflip skill matchup — whoever manages spheres and cooldowns better wins the trade, since neither of you has a clean advantage yet. From levels 2-5 your full combo edges her out as long as you avoid her stun setup, since her combo needs a landed sphere-stun to punish you first.","mid":"Your combo advantage holds through level 5, but the instant she hits 6 the range and burst on Unleashed Power flip the fight in her favor if she has spheres up — respect that spike and don't extend trades into her stun range. By 2+ items her longer effective range starts winning poke wars outright.","late":"Late Syndra outranges and outbursts you: Splinters make Force of Will partly true damage so MR does not answer it, and Unleashed Power executes below a threshold from 675 range. Force short windows on her R cooldown or catch her without spheres rather than trading poke. Play around vision.","whys":["Level 1 is an even skill matchup with neither side holding a clean structural edge","Your combo damage edges ahead once you have enough spells to threaten her before her stun lands","Continued combo advantage as long as you avoid walking into her sphere stun setup","Mid-level combo strength still favors you provided her spheres aren't in position","Her level 6 Unleashed Power grants huge range and burst that flips the fight if she has spheres ready","Combo damage returns to your favor in short trades once you've weathered her ultimate cooldown","Full-build Syndra's superior range and burst wins straight poke exchanges late game"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"taliyah","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Full combo LeBlanc bursts Taliyah down before her Seismic Shove and Unraveled Earth can chain into meaningful damage, since her own combo needs both worked into position first."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Mimic-Chain deletes her from range and her Weaver's Wall ultimate is more of a repositioning tool than a burst answer, so you still win the all-in."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Taliyah roams constantly off cooldowns rather than fighting you directly, so punishing her push-and-roam pattern matters more than raw trades."},{"when":"Late","text":"Fully scaled Taliyah's map-wide roaming and wall zoning make her a nightmare for your team's positioning even though she still loses straight 1v1 burst trades to you."}],"wants":{"you":["Poke and punish her when she over-commits to landing E-Q","Deny her roam windows by pushing waves and warding river","Burst her fast in short trades since her combo is slow to set up"],"foe":["Roam mid-to-side lanes using Q resets off minions/walls","Farm safely and avoid direct all-ins she loses","Use ultimate walls late to block pursuit and control skirmishes"]},"early":"Levels 1-2 you outpoke and outtrade her cleanly — Taliyah's combo needs real setup time and she has no answer to your Sigil range, so press every wave. She'll likely play passive and look to roam instead of trading directly.","mid":"Levels 3-6 your full combo still kills her outright, but never W through Unraveled Earth — a dash across her minefield stuns you and hands her the trade. Wait for Seismic Shove to be spent, then chain and detonate. She leans on roaming since she loses the 1v1, so track her river paths and ward to deny picks.","late":"Even at full build Taliyah loses straight burst trades to you, but Weaver's Wall at 120s is terrain: it cuts off your W return and your flank angle outright. Pick your entry before she casts it, focus her fast in fights, and never path through her mines to get there.","whys":["You dominate early trades since Taliyah needs setup time she simply doesn't have against your poke","Continued lane control as her combo still can't threaten you before it's fully assembled","Full combo burst kills her outright before her own damage lands","Combo advantage holds — wait out her 14-second Shove, then step up and detonate","Mimic-Chain burst still wins the all-in even after she hits her ultimate","Item power continues to favor your faster burst over her roam-reliant kit","Full-build combo damage still favors you outright in a straight 1v1 trade"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"talon","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Full combo LeBlanc bursts Talon down before his Rake into Noxian Diplomacy all-in lands, since your 700-range poke chips him while his leap only reaches 575."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Mimic-Chain kills him from range even through Shadow Assault, since your burst still outpaces the damage he can land while stealthed."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Talon roams constantly rather than trading in lane, so punishing his push-and-leave pattern matters more than direct fights."},{"when":"Late","text":"Fully-fed Talon one-shots backline carries once Blade's End hits three stacks and bleeds, but he still loses straight 1v1 trades to your burst."}],"wants":{"you":["Poke him down before he can wall-jump into range","Push waves fast to deny his roam timers","Ward river to see his Shadow Assault flanks coming"],"foe":["Roam side lanes off wave clears since he loses lane trades","Vault a wall into a Q leap only when you're low or overextended","Look for stealth flank kills on backline carries once fed"]},"early":"Levels 1-2 you outrange and outpoke him cleanly — his Q leaps only from 575 and Rake alone doesn't threaten your HP. Keep him off CS with Sigil poke at 700 and force him to walk into your chain to do anything.","mid":"Levels 3-6 your full combo still kills him first even through his wall-vault all-in, and Mimic-Chain finishes from range before Shadow Assault adds up. Step up whenever Rake is spent — 9s at rank 1 — and never stand inside 575, where Noxian Diplomacy crits instead of leaping. He'll abandon lane for roams, so ward river.","late":"Even scaled up, Talon still loses a straight 1v1 to your ranged burst, but a fed Talon roaming with Shadow Assault can pick off isolated carries in side lanes or through vision gaps. Group with your team, avoid isolated positioning, and continue winning any direct confrontation with him.","whys":["You dominate early trades since his only gap-closer is an 8-second Q leap from just 575 range","Continued poke advantage as his all-in still requires setup he can't safely get","Full combo burst kills him before his own combo can finish, even through his gap-close","Mid-level combo strength still favors you as his damage output hasn't caught up","Mimic-Chain burst wins the fight even through his stealth engage at level 6","Item power continues to favor your ranged burst over his melee-reliant all-in","Full-build combo damage still wins the straight 1v1 trade against his kit"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"twistedfate","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Once you have the sigil and E-Q online, you punish TF's stiff Wild Cards wind-up and no-mobility base kit hard — he can't step up to trade without eating a full combo."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Your full-combo one-shot threat plus Distortion re-position beats his level 6 Destiny pick, since Gold Card stun into Destiny doesn't out-damage your burst if you land first."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Lost Chapter/Shadowflame timing lets you clear waves faster and roam off Distortion before his Destiny comes off cooldown, denying his global pressure window."},{"when":"Late","text":"TF's Destiny-enabled pick potential and his gold-card burst on carries become the bigger threat once he's relevant to teamfights, so one-shot him before that 110-second ult sets up on your backline."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait Wild Cards on its 6s cooldown, then commit E-Q","Freeze or slow-push to deny his easy last-hits under tower","Track his Destiny cooldown before diving/roaming","Use Distortion to dodge Gold Card stun windows"],"foe":["Land Wild Card poke to zone your melee-range combo","Save Destiny to counter-gank or punish your roams","Farm safely and cash Loaded Dice gold into items","Group around his Destiny global pick threat at 6+"]},"early":"You out-trade TF at levels 1-2 as long as you respect his Wild Card poke — bait it with your W clone, then punish the long cooldown with Sigil-empowered Q-E all-in. His card-flip randomness means he often can't guarantee Blue Card damage, so most exchanges before level 3 favor your burst combo if you play from max range.","mid":"Levels 3-6 are your strongest window: Distortion lets you dash in, burst, and blink back out before Gold Card can land its stun at 200 range, and the sigil detonation chips faster than his Loaded Dice gold buys back. At 6, Destiny grants global vision and pick threat on a 170-second cooldown, but you still win any 1v1 if you catch him without a card ready — bait the flip, then all-in.","late":"Once TF hits his first item and Destiny drops to 110 seconds, his job shifts to setting up picks across the map rather than dueling you, so your late task is denying him easy Destiny angles by warding and grouping early. In teamfights you still delete him in one rotation if you flank before his gold card connects — but remember Destiny reveals every champion, so it strips your angle too.","whys":["You outrange and out-burst TF's early auto-trades since his Wild Cards windup telegraphs and your combo punishes any misstep","Sigil-empowered Q-E all-in beats his card-flip poke because his damage floor is too low without guaranteed Blue Card","Distortion lets you dodge Gold Card and reposition faster than he can reset, keeping trades one-sided","Same logic holds through levels 4-5 — your kill threshold arrives before his damage scales enough to punish your dive","Your burst combo one-shots him before his level-6 Destiny pick setup matters in a direct fight","First item Distortion cooldown lets you roam and punish his lane before his own Destiny window resets","Your assassin burst still deletes him in one flanking rotation even once he's fully itemized for pick potential"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"veigar","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Your mobility and burst punish Veigar's total lack of escape — once you have E-Q, you can dash in on Event Horizon cooldown and kill him before his cage even matters."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Sigil into Ethereal Chains into Distortion threatens lethal on a squishy immobile mage, and your Distortion re-position dodges his cage-into-Dark Matter setup entirely if you engage off cooldown."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Shadowflame timing lets you one-shot him before Event Horizon forms or Primordial Burst stacks matter, since his damage needs a landed stun you can simply walk around."},{"when":"Late","text":"Veigar's stacking Phenomenal Evil Power scaling eventually threatens to one-shot squishies including you, so late you must kill him first or bait his cage before committing."}],"wants":{"you":["Punish his zero-mobility with all-in windows on his key cooldowns","Bait Event Horizon cage before committing your combo","Deny his Q stacks by not standing near minions unnecessarily","Track his stun combo cooldown before diving"],"foe":["Land Dark Matter poke to safely stack AP from range","Cage-stun you with Event Horizon to enable his team","Farm safely behind cage threat zone","Scale into a one-shot late-game Rabadon's Deathcap threat"]},"early":"You dominate the 1-2 trade because Veigar has no dash, no blink, no way to escape your all-in — make him throw Baleful Strike at the wave, then step in with E-Q. His stack-based scaling means he's weak early relative to you, and most exchanges before level 3 favor your mobility outright. Deny the stacks where you can: every minion that Q kills is permanent AP.","mid":"Levels 3-6 remain heavily yours: Distortion lets you dodge Event Horizon's cage placement and burst him down before Primordial Burst turns threatening, since your combo one-shots him well before his own damage window opens. Even at 6 his cage-into-Dark Matter setup needs a landed stun first, and if you chain him at 925 from outside the 725 cage, on his downtime, you simply kill him.","late":"Veigar's late-game one-shot potential off stacked AP is real, but it needs a landed cage first — deny that by baiting Event Horizon from max range or opening at 925, outside its 725 radius, so he never sets up. Watch your own health bar too: Primordial Burst scales on missing health, so being chipped is exactly what makes him lethal.","whys":["Veigar has zero mobility so your early combo lands nearly every all-in he can't escape","Event Horizon is 20s at rank 1, and that gap is a free window to dash in and burst him","Distortion lets you dodge Event Horizon placement entirely, keeping the trade one-sided in your favor","The mobility gap only widens at levels 4-5 as your kill threshold arrives before his stack scaling catches up","Your one-shot combo kills him before his level-6 cage-into-Dark Matter setup ever lands if you engage first","First item Shadowflame burst kills him before Primordial Burst and his stacks become relevant","Even with a late one-shot threat of his own, you delete him first if you open from outside his 725 cage"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"vex","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"You still out-trade Vex once you have E-Q, punishing her lack of a hard escape, but her Mistral Bolt slow into Personal Space damage bites back if you engage on cooldown."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Your full-combo burst threatens to kill her before her Shadow Surge dash punishes your dash-in with its bonus damage, so you need Distortion up to engage safely around her ultimate."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Shadowflame lets you one-shot her from range, but force Personal Space out first — a 16s shield is what eats an opener that would otherwise kill."},{"when":"Late","text":"Vex's Doom 'n Gloom fear interrupts any dash or blink you engage with, Distortion included, so late-game you must bait the fear proc before committing your combo."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait Mistral Bolt before dashing in with E-Q","Avoid using Distortion into an active Gloom fear window","Punish her immobility outside of Shadow Surge cooldown","Track Shadow Surge, 140s at rank 1, before all-ins"],"foe":["Land Mistral Bolt to slow and poke from safety","Punish your dash-ins with Gloom's fear-on-dash passive","Use Shadow Surge to close distance or peel","Recast Shadow Surge to dash onto a low-health target"]},"early":"You out-trade Vex in the 1-2 window since her poke requires landing Mistral Bolt and her damage without stacks is low, letting your E-Q punish her whenever the bolt misses. Respect her Personal Space passive punishing melee range, but from max range your burst wins clean before she has the mana or cooldowns to fight back.","mid":"Levels 3-6 stay in your favor because her only real escape, Shadow Surge, is also her main engage tool, so if you catch her without it up you kill her outright with Distortion-enabled bursts. The one thing to respect is her Gloom passive fearing you on dash-ins, which can flip an engage badly if you commit Distortion into an active fear window — bait it first.","late":"Vex's Shadow Surge becomes a real late-game threat, marking you from 3000 and dashing straight in, and Personal Space still shields the opener. Your job is baiting her fear proc before committing your engage, since dashing blind into Doom 'n Gloom interrupts the dash outright and turns your kill combo into a trade you lose.","whys":["Vex's poke requires a landed Mistral Bolt, and misses leave her open to your E-Q punish at levels 1-2","Her low base damage without stacks means you win most direct trades once you have your full combo online","Distortion lets you dash safely around her lack of escape as long as you dodge her Gloom fear window","The same mobility gap holds through levels 4-5 since she still needs Shadow Surge to threaten you back","Your burst still kills her before her ultimate Shadow Surge dash can punish your engage, if you catch her without it","First item Shadowflame burst one-shots her from range before Gloom stacks turn her fear into a real threat","Even with her ultimate scaling, your combo still kills her first if you bait the fear proc before committing"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"viktor","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Viktor"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Once you have E-Q, you punish Viktor's slow, no-mobility laning stance — his Siphon Power shield only reduces damage, it doesn't stop your burst from threatening a kill."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Sigil-Ethereal Chains threatens lethal, and the chain cancels Arcane Storm outright — catch him with Gravity Field on its 17s and you win the direct fight."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Shadowflame lets you burst him from stealth before his Hex Core evolutions (especially E evolve) start punishing your engage range with extra slow and zone control."},{"when":"Late","text":"Once Viktor has banked enough Hex Fragments to augment his kit and his Arcane Storm zones entire fights, his AoE and area denial outscale your single-target burst, flipping the matchup his way."}],"wants":{"you":["Dash in before Gravity Field zone is set","Punish his low mobility with all-in windows off cooldown","Burst him before his Hex Core evolves fully","Deny his safe wave clear by contesting freezes"],"foe":["Zone with Gravity Field to punish your dash-ins","Land Hextech Ray poke for safe chip damage","Augment Hextech Ray for its trailing explosion","Use Arcane Storm to zone teamfights late"]},"early":"You win the 1-2 trade because Viktor has no dash or blink and his Siphon Power only reduces incoming damage rather than escaping it — bait his Death Ray poke, then punish with E-Q before he can reposition. His early damage is low relative to his scaling, so most exchanges before level 3 favor your burst if you engage from an angle he can't punish with Gravity Field.","mid":"Levels 3-6 stay in your favor since Distortion lets you dash around his Gravity Field zone and burst him before Arcane Storm matters — and if he does cast the storm, Ethereal Chains cancels the channel outright. Gravity Field is 17s at rank 1: the moment it drops, that window is yours to step into and kill him in.","late":"Viktor flips the matchup once his augments are banked and Arcane Storm zones out entire fights with sustained AoE damage — his area denial and the Gravity Field safety net start beating your single-target burst. Play flanks and picks rather than straight engages, and chain him the instant he starts the storm: the channel dies to your E.","whys":["Viktor has no dash or blink at levels 1-2, so your burst punishes him whenever Hextech Ray poke misses or Gravity Field isn't set","His early damage is low relative to scaling, letting your E-Q all-in win most direct trades before level 3","Distortion lets you dash around Gravity Field and burst him before Arcane Storm becomes a threat","The mobility gap still favors you at levels 4-5 as your kill threshold arrives before his Hex Core evolutions catch up","Your one-shot combo still kills him at level 6 if you catch him without Arcane Storm or Gravity Field ready","First item burst kills him from stealth before his evolved Hex Core adds enough control to punish your engage","Once Arcane Storm zones full fights and his augments are banked, his AoE damage and safety net start beating your single-target burst"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"vladimir","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Vladimir"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Once you have E-Q, you can punish Vlad before he hits full Crimson Pact scaling, but respect that Sanguine Pool dodges your entire combo if he pools on reaction."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Your sigil burst threatens to kill him before Hemoplague amplifies his team's damage, but a held Sanguine Pool sidesteps the all-in — force it, count 28s, then go."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Shadowflame plus an early Oblivion Orb matters here — without Grievous Wounds his no-cost Transfusion heals back most of what your first-item burst takes off."},{"when":"Late","text":"Crimson Pact converts every 30 bonus Health into 1 AP, so his health items are damage too, and Hemoplague's 120s amp plus per-champion heal wins the extended fights your single burst window cannot reach."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait Sanguine Pool before committing your full combo","Punish him hard before his Crimson Pact stacking kicks in","Burst before Hemoplague timer amplifies incoming damage on your team","Deny safe wave farming with pressure and roams"],"foe":["Pool through your engage to negate the burst","Buy health items that Crimson Pact converts into AP","Use Hemoplague to enable team all-ins","Farm safely with Tides of Blood ranged poke"]},"early":"You win the 1-2 trade since Vlad's Crimson Pact scaling hasn't kicked in yet and his only real defensive tool, Sanguine Pool, has a real cooldown you can bait out with your W clone before committing E-Q. His damage output early is low relative to yours, so most exchanges before level 3 favor a well-timed all-in if you force the pool first.","mid":"Levels 3-6 remain in your favor because Distortion's 4-second return outlasts his 2-second pool: enter, let him submerge, and be there when he surfaces with 28 seconds of nothing. Hemoplague is 120s and heals him for every champion it hits, so never let him cast it into your whole side — chain him first, or fight after it expires.","late":"Vlad flips the matchup late as Crimson Pact scaling off stacked health and AP items turns him into a genuine skirmish threat, and Hemoplague's team-wide execute amplifies every extended fight in his favor. Your one-shot burst window shrinks relative to his sustain, so late-game you need to pick him off with stealth flanks rather than committing to prolonged trades.","whys":["Vlad's Crimson Pact hasn't scaled yet at levels 1-2, so your burst wins trades if you bait Sanguine Pool before all-inning","His low early damage output still favors your combo through this window as long as the pool is on cooldown","Distortion lets you re-engage after his pool, effectively negating his one escape if you bait it out first","The same logic holds at levels 4-5 since your kill threshold still arrives before his sustain fully scales up","Your burst still kills him at level 6 if you land it before Hemoplague's amplification timer starts ticking","First item burst kills him before Crimson Pact bonus AP from health items makes his sustained damage scarier","Once Crimson Pact scaling and Hemoplague team execute threat come online, his sustain starts beating your single burst window"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"xerath","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Once you have E-Q, you punish Xerath's total lack of mobility outside a short root — if you close the gap through his poke, he has almost nothing to stop your all-in."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Your burst threatens lethal before Rite of the Arcane matters in lane, and your chain cancels that channel outright — Distortion closes the gap straight through Arcanopulse poke."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Shadowflame lets you one-shot him from stealth the moment you're in range, since his kit has no answer once you're on top of him."},{"when":"Late","text":"Xerath's 5000-range Rite of the Arcane punishes teamfights, but the channel holds him still — chain it and he dies, and up close he dies to your burst anyway."}],"wants":{"you":["Close distance through his poke using Distortion","Punish his immobility hard once you're in melee range","Deny his safe farming under tower with pressure","Flank from stealth to avoid his long-range poke entirely"],"foe":["Poke from max range with Arcanopulse and Eye of Destruction","Root you to prevent your engage","Snipe from 5000 range with Rite of the Arcane","Kite backward and channel Rite of the Arcane safely"]},"early":"Xerath's poke is real but his complete lack of mobility means once you close the gap with E-Q, he has no way to disengage, so bait his stun with your W clone then dash in. Most exchanges before level 3 favor you as long as you play around Shocking Orb's 13-second cooldown and don't walk into Arcanopulse blind.","mid":"Levels 3-6 stay heavily in your favor since Distortion lets you close his siege range faster than he can kite, and once you're on top of him your burst kills him outright — his kit has no answer to a mobile assassin in his face. Rite of the Arcane is a 130-second channel: hold Ethereal Chains for it and he loses the ultimate and the fight.","late":"Xerath's teamfight poke and Rite of the Arcane from 5000 range make him dangerous late, but that threat evaporates the moment you close distance — and the ultimate is a channel your Ethereal Chains cancels outright. Respect Arcanopulse on the approach, but the duel, and the counter-engage, both remain yours.","whys":["Xerath's total lack of mobility at levels 1-2 means your E-Q all-in punishes him hard once you close distance through his poke","His only defensive tool is Shocking Orb on a 13-second cooldown, so direct trades favor your burst once you're in range","Distortion lets you close his siege range faster than he can kite, and he has no answer once you're on top of him","The mobility gap only widens at levels 4-5 since your kill threshold arrives well before his immobility issue resolves","Your burst still one-shots him at level 6, and Ethereal Chains cancels Rite of the Arcane outright the moment he starts the channel","First item burst kills him instantly from stealth the moment you're in melee range with no counterplay available","Even with strong teamfight poke and global execute range late, closing the gap still lets your burst kill him outright"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"yasuo","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Yasuo","LeBlanc"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Once you have E-Q, you punish Yasuo's low base damage and telegraphed Sweeping Blade dash before he stacks his shield off farming, so ranged poke into all-in wins clean."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"At 6 Yasuo's Last Breath combined with a jungler or support knockup threatens to delete you instantly, and Wind Wall eats Ethereal Chains outright, flipping the all-in against you if he catches the engage timing."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Shadowflame lets you burst him down again before his crit-scaling Steel Tempest damage ramps up, since your one-shot threat returns once you outscale his early snowball."},{"when":"Late","text":"Yasuo's crit-item scaling and Last Breath execute in teamfights becomes a serious backline threat late, but if you engage him alone away from setup, your burst still wins the direct duel."}],"wants":{"you":["Poke from range before committing your all-in","Bait Wind Wall before landing Chaos Orb","Avoid all-in when he has Last Breath and a knockup ally nearby","Punish his Steel Tempest wind-up before shield stacks"],"foe":["Farm safely behind Steel Tempest wave clear","Wind Wall your Ethereal Chains engage","Combo with jungler knockup into Last Breath","Snowball crit items for scaling teamfight presence"]},"early":"You out-trade Yasuo in the 1-2 window since his damage without stacked crit is low and Steel Tempest's wind-up telegraphs your dodge — bait his wave clear pattern, then punish with E-Q once his shield is down. Most exchanges before level 3 favor your burst as long as you don't feed him easy Sweeping Blade resets on minions.","mid":"Levels 3-5 you still win clean trades and all-ins, but level 6 flips briefly — his Last Breath combined with any external knockup threatens an instant burst on you, and Wind Wall eats Ethereal Chains for its full 4 seconds, so never open blind at 6. Bait the wall from outside his 400 range first, then engage once it's down.","late":"Yasuo's crit scaling and Last Breath execute make him a genuine teamfight threat on your backline late, but in an isolated duel away from setup your burst combo still kills him first. Focus on picking him off with stealth flanks rather than fighting him inside his own Last Breath-knockup combo window.","whys":["Yasuo's low damage without stacked crit at levels 1-2 lets your combo win most direct trades before his shield or crit chance ramps up","Steel Tempest's telegraphed wind-up gives you a dodge window, keeping the level 1-2 trade in your favor","The same low early damage still favors your all-in through levels 4-5 as long as his shield is down when you engage","At level 6 his Last Breath paired with a knockup ally threatens to burst you down first, and Wind Wall can block your key engage tool if timed well","Shadowflame burst kills him again before his crit-scaling Steel Tempest starts hitting meaningfully harder","Your combo still wins an isolated duel away from his setup even as his crit items and Last Breath scale into a teamfight threat","In a straight 1v1 without his knockup partner, your burst still kills him first despite his growing late-game power"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"yone","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Yone","LeBlanc"],"spikes":[{"when":"Lvl 3-5","text":"Chain W-Distortion into Q-Sigil and E-Ethereal Chains the instant Yone sends Soul Unbound forward; the spirit is no dash and the body stays put, so punish the body he snaps back to and leave inside its 22-second window."},{"when":"Lvl 6","text":"Yone hits 6 with a genuine all-in via R-Fate Sealed knock-up into full auto-crit follow-up; you have to respect flash-R range and not facecheck a trading window."},{"when":"1st item","text":"Lost Chapter into Shadow Flame lets you two-item burst him from range before Yone's Kraken/Wit's End spike lets him trade back in melee; keep poking, never let him walk you down."},{"when":"Late","text":"Your burst chunks him from range through fog before he can close on Mortal Steel, but Fate Sealed reaches 1000 and drags everyone in its line, so never stack up behind a target he can cut through."}],"wants":{"you":["Bait his E-Soul Unbound before you commit Q","Hold W-Distortion until Mortal Steel's knockup is spent","Poke wave with Q from max range pre-6","Track his ult timer once he hits 6"],"foe":["Send Soul Unbound in without risking his body","Wait for you to overextend post-Q cooldown","All-in immediately after hitting 6 with R up","Farm safely under tower if poke is losing him CS"]},"early":"Levels 1-2 you out-range him completely — Sigil Mark poke into Distortion picks off his last-hits for free since Yone's E has a long cooldown and no reliable answer to ranged chip damage. He has to walk up with basic attacks to farm, which is exactly the window you punish with Q-E combos before backing off.","mid":"Levels 3-5 stay yours: bait Soul Unbound, land your full combo on the body he leaves behind, then reset with W before the spirit snaps home. At level 6 the fight briefly swings his way — Fate Sealed blinks behind the last champion in the line and drags everyone hit back to him, chaining into crit autos that outdamage your one combo, so play around a 120-second ult timer rather than trading greedily.","late":"Once you're two items deep (Lost Chapter/Shadowflame into Luden's or Cryptbloom) your burst from max range outpaces anything Yone can do without closing on Mortal Steel, letting you poke him out of teamfights before he ever gets to auto you. Stand off the line in skirmishes — Fate Sealed reaches 1000 and pulls every champion it clips, so the safe spot is not directly behind your frontline.","whys":["Yone's E has a long cooldown at level 1 and he can't punish your ranged Sigil Mark poke, so you control minute one for free","You out-trade his cooldown-locked kit at level 2 with repeatable Q pokes he can't answer without overextending","Bait-and-punish Soul Unbound defines level 3 — it is 16s at rank 3, and it is no dash, so he cannot leave your combo","Your combo damage and mobility edge holds through levels 4-5 as his cooldowns still lag behind your poke frequency","Yone's level 6 all-in with Fate Sealed pulling you back into crit autos out-damages your single-rotation burst if he catches you","You regain the edge on First item — Lost Chapter/Shadow Flame burst outranges anything he can do without landing E first","Late-game 2+ items your ranged burst wins skirmishes before he closes distance, so long as he hasn't banked a free ult reset"]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"zed","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Zed","Zed","Zed"],"spikes":[{"when":"Level 2","text":"LeBlanc's Sigil-Q burst punishes Zed hard before he has Shadow shuriken-Q reliably poking and before Living Shadow is up at 6."},{"when":"Level 6","text":"LeBlanc can still out-burst Zed briefly at 6 since her Mimic-doubled chains land before his ultimate combo is fully optimized."},{"when":"Zed first item (Eclipse)","text":"Zed's Eclipse spike gives him the burst and sustain from the shield proc to all-in and kill LeBlanc before her combo lands."},{"when":"Zed 3-item (Edge of Night/LDR)","text":"A fed Zed with an Edge of Night spellshield eats your chain, then goes untargetable through your answer on a 100-second Death Mark."}],"wants":{"you":["Punish Zed before Eclipse spike","Burst before his ult combo is smooth","Poke down his energy early","Play safe once he hits Eclipse"],"foe":["Farm safely under poke","Rush Eclipse for all-in power","Wait out your combo inside Death Mark","Snowball into split-push threat"]},"early":"LeBlanc wins the earliest levels because her Sigil-Q trade deals more upfront damage than Zed's shuriken poke, and Zed's energy costs make him hesitant to spam early harass. LeBlanc should shove and punish him whenever he pushes forward without Living Shadow available. Zed is forced into a farming pattern early, just trying to reach level 6 and his first item without falling too far behind.","mid":"LeBlanc can still edge out level 6 trades briefly with her doubled chains giving her a reliable lockdown, but the moment Zed completes his Eclipse spike, the matchup flips hard in his favor since his Death Mark burst combined with the shield proc lets him all-in and kill LeBlanc before her own combo finishes. LeBlanc must play very cautiously around this item timing and avoid overextending. Zed's game plan is simply to rush Eclipse and look for all-in windows the instant he has it.","late":"With a scaled build including Edge of Night for spellshield-protected engages and Lord Dominik's for armor penetration, Zed becomes a terrifying pick threat who eats your chain behind the shield, opens from fog with a Living Shadow swap, and goes untargetable through your answer with Death Mark. Your combo is telegraphed and squishy, giving Zed every advantage in a prolonged 1v1 or pick scenario. This makes Zed the clear favorite once both champions are fully itemized.","whys":["LeBlanc's Sigil-Q burst out-trades Zed's energy-limited shuriken poke at levels 1-2.","LeBlanc continues to punish Zed through levels 3-5 while his energy costs limit his aggression.","At level 6, LeBlanc's Mimic-doubled Chains still lands before Zed's ultimate combo is fully optimized, keeping her ahead.","LeBlanc's first item burst window lets her punish Zed just before his Eclipse spike flips the all-in math.","Zed's Eclipse spike gives him the shield-and-burst power to all-in and kill LeBlanc before her combo lands.","Zed's Death Mark burst combined with Living Shadow repositioning outclasses LeBlanc's telegraphed combo in the mid-to-late transition.","Zed's fully itemized burst, Edge of Night spellshield, and untargetable Death Mark entry make him the dominant pick-threat late."]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"ziggs","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc"],"spikes":[{"when":"Level 2","text":"LeBlanc's mobility lets her dodge Ziggs's slow skillshots and punish him hard with Sigil-Q since he has zero escape."},{"when":"Level 6","text":"LeBlanc's doubled chains give her a guaranteed root to close the gap on Ziggs before his ultimate can even be considered defensively."},{"when":"Ziggs first item (Liandry's)","text":"Ziggs's poke damage ramps up significantly, but he still has no way to actually punish LeBlanc's dive once she's on top of him."},{"when":"Full build","text":"Even at full build his only exit is a 12-second Satchel Charge that knocks him away — bait it out, then Distortion in while it is down and he has nothing left."}],"wants":{"you":["Close the gap with W blink","Dodge his skillshot poke","Burst him down with no counterplay","Roam off his lack of pressure"],"foe":["Poke from max range","Zone with minefields","Avoid melee range entirely","Hope for peel from teammates"]},"early":"You dominate the early levels because Ziggs has no dash and his only escape, Satchel Charge, is 20 seconds at rank 1. Dodge the Bouncing Bomb poke with your mobility and dive whenever he is out of position. Short Fuse arms an empowered auto every few seconds, so his wind-up is your cue to step out, not the bomb cast. He plays at maximum range and hopes Hexplosive Minefield deters the all-in.","mid":"The pressure continues into level 6 and beyond, since Mimic-doubled Ethereal Chains gives you two roots inside a single Satchel Charge cooldown and he cannot knock himself away from both. Snowball hard and roam off the pressure this lane generates, since Ziggs offers little in return. His real hope is peel from allies, turret proximity, and a satchel he saved rather than spent on the wave.","late":"Even at a full build, Ziggs remains completely immobile with no innate escape besides his ultimate's minor knockback utility, meaning LeBlanc's gap-closing W blink continues to make him one of the easiest targets in the game to pick off in skirmishes. His poke damage is real from a distance, but any time LeBlanc can reach him one-on-one the fight is essentially over instantly. This matchup remains one of LeBlanc's most favorable across the entire game.","whys":["LeBlanc's mobility lets her dodge Ziggs's poke and punish him hard at levels 1-2 since he has zero escape.","LeBlanc continues to dominate through levels 3-5 as Ziggs cannot punish her aggression in any way.","At level 6, LeBlanc's doubled Chains guarantee a root that closes the gap before Ziggs can react defensively.","LeBlanc's first item burst is more than enough to kill Ziggs the moment she reaches him.","LeBlanc's snowballing kill pressure and lack of counterplay from Ziggs keep her dominant through the mid-game.","LeBlanc's overwhelming lead and gap-closing tools remain favored heading into late game.","Ziggs owns one escape on a 12-second Satchel Charge, so full-build burst still kills him the moment it has been spent."]},
  {"a":"leblanc_mid","b":"zoe","win":["LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","LeBlanc","Zoe"],"spikes":[{"when":"Level 2","text":"LeBlanc's Sigil-Q burst out-trades Zoe before Zoe has bubble-sleep landing consistently or enough passive procs from missiles."},{"when":"Level 6","text":"LeBlanc's doubled chains root shuts down Zoe's sleep-into-burst combo before she can use Portal Jump to reposition."},{"when":"Zoe level 6","text":"Zoe's Portal Jump combined with a landed sleep bubble threatens a full burst combo that can delete LeBlanc from range."},{"when":"Zoe 3-item (Shadowflame/Void Staff)","text":"Zoe's long-range burst poke and pick potential with sleep bubble let her one-shot LeBlanc from safety before she can even close distance."}],"wants":{"you":["Punish before sleep lands","Burst before Portal Jump repositions her","Poke down her early HP","Deny her Spell Thief pickups"],"foe":["Land sleep bubble for burst","Farm safely with Paddle Star! poke","Use Portal Jump defensively","Snowball into pick-heavy late game"]},"early":"You hold the edge in the earliest levels because your Sigil-Q trade is more consistent and less telegraphed than Zoe's Paddle Star!, which she redirects in flight but which only hits hard after a long straight run. Punish every missed Sleepy Trouble Bubble immediately — it is 18 seconds at rank 1. Press while her combo potential is low; she has to poke cautiously and avoid trades she cannot capitalize on yet.","mid":"Through level 6 and your first item you keep the advantage by using Mimic'd chains to lock Zoe down before she lands the sleep-into-burst combo or repositions with Portal Jump — which is a one-second blink out and back, so chain her on the return, not the exit. This is your peak window to snowball, since Zoe is extremely squishy. Her counterplay is poking from extreme range and committing only on a clear bubble angle.","late":"Once Zoe scales into a full item build, her long-range burst combo becomes capable of deleting LeBlanc from well outside the range LeBlanc can retaliate, especially if the sleep bubble connects first and locks LeBlanc down for the follow-up. LeBlanc's own combo requires closing distance, which is exactly the risk Zoe's kit is designed to punish at range. This makes Zoe the safer, higher-ceiling late-game pick threat in prolonged skirmishes and picks.","whys":["LeBlanc's Sigil-Q burst out-trades Zoe at levels 1-2 before her sleep bubble lands consistently.","LeBlanc continues to punish Zoe through levels 3-5 while her combo remains inconsistent.","At level 6, LeBlanc's Mimic-doubled Chains shuts down Zoe's sleep-into-burst combo before Portal Jump can reposition her.","LeBlanc's first item burst window still lets her all-in and kill Zoe before her damage output catches up.","LeBlanc's ability to punish Zoe's whiffed bubble attempts keeps her ahead through the mid-game.","LeBlanc's snowballed lead and burst reliability remain favored heading into late game.","Zoe's long-range sleep-into-burst combo lets her delete LeBlanc from safety before she can close distance once fully itemized."]}
